\par\par \b \uc2 India adviser: 'All options' to handle inflows surge, volatility\b0\par\par\uc2 421 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 November 2010\par 09:07\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * As of now, not in favour of imposing direct controls\par \par \uc2 * India can absorb present level of inflows\par \par \uc2 * Current account deficit largely funded by inflows (Adds quotes, background)\par \par \uc2 By Manoj Kumar\par \par \uc2 NEW DELHI, Nov 4 (Reuters) - India will keep all options on the table to handle a surge of capital inflows and the attendant volatitily, but is not in favor of direct controls, the Finance Ministry's chief economic adviser said on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 The comments came a day after the U.S. Federal Reserve committed to buy $600 billion more in government bonds by June 2011, a move many fear will lead to a surge of investment inflows into emerging markets like India, Korea, Brazil and China.\par \par \uc2 "Part of the money is also coming in because of the confidence in India's ability to grow," Chief Economic Adviser to the Finance Ministry Kaushik Basu said.\par \par \uc2 "So we have to keep all options on the table about how we handle it in case there is a surge and volatility."\par \par \uc2 India however faces a huge current account deficit which is nearing almost 3 percent of GDP, a gap which is largely financed by capital inflows. Policymakers therefore say India does not need to impose capital controls right now.\par \par \uc2 "India is a much stronger economy today and so we should be able to take it," Basu said.\par \par \uc2 India has already received more than $26 bilion of capital inflows into equities so far this year.\par \par \uc2 India's response has been measured in comparison to other economies like China, South Korea and Brazil who have vowed to fight the latest round of quantitative easing by the United States. [ID:nTOE6A300V]\par \par \uc2 Central banks in countries like Japan and South Korea that are faced with a rising local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  have indicated they may take measures to counter the appreciation pressure.\par \par \uc2 India's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the \uc2 Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 , has however maintained it will only \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets if inflows turn 'lumpy' or 'volatile'.\par \par \uc2 The Indian rupee <INR=IN> has appreciated 5.1 percent in 2010.\par \par \uc2 However, on the global front, Wednesdays move by the Fed could have negative repercussions for the forthcoming Group of 20 meeting in Seoul.\par \par \uc2 With many emerging countries willing to retaliate, government officials say a meaningful outcome to the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and global imbalance debate now looks a little bleak in Seoul. (Editing by Bryson Hull)\par \par \uc2 INDIA-ECONOMY/ADVISOR (UPDATE 1)|LANGEN|SNS|SNI|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|RNP|DNP   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020101104e6b4000u7\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
