\par\par \b \uc2 =DJ UPDATE: India Sept Exports Up 23.2%; High Commodity Prices, Low Base Help\b0\par\par\uc2 501 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 Oktober 2010\par 12:07\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2010, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 (Adds comments of trade minister, details)\par \par \uc2 By Prasanta Sahu and Anant Vijay Kala\par \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES\par \par \uc2 NEW DELHI (Dow Jones)--India's September exports rose sharply helped by high global commodity prices and a weak base in the previous year, keeping the country on track to meet its export target despite a strong local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Exports during the month rose 23.2% to $18.02 billion from a year ago, Trade Secretary Rahul Khullar said Monday.\par \par \uc2 In August, exports were up 22.5% at $16.64 billion.\par \par \uc2 Exports till September from the start of the fiscal year on April 1 increased 27.6% to $103.3 billion, Khullar told reporters at a conference.\par \par \uc2 Imports in September rose 26.1% to $27.14 billion, while between April-September they were 29.9% higher at $166.5 billion.\par \par \uc2 The trade deficit in September narrowed to $9.12 billion from August's $13.06 billion as imports moderated. The trade gap was $63.2 billion in April-Sept.\par \par \uc2 "In the first six months (of the fiscal year) we have done well," said Minister for Commerce and Industry Anand Sharma. "We are very much on track to meet our target of the 15% increase (in exports) and India's exports to cross $200 billion this (fiscal) year," Sharma added.\par \par \uc2 India's export growth slowed for the first time in nine months in July after expanding more than 30% each month between November and June largely because of weaker base year levels at the time of the global economic crisis.\par \par \uc2 Khullar said the favorable base will likely continue for three to four more months, aiding export growth.\par \par \uc2 Also, global festive demand in November and December will likely raise consumption overseas and could further spur demand for Indian exports.\par \par \uc2 But a recent spike in the rupee could dampen prospects for Indian exporters.\par \par \uc2 Trade Secretary Khullar said a strong rupee could hurt exporters with a time lag.\par \par \uc2 The rupee has risen 1.76% against the U.S. dollar since Sept. 28, driven by heavy foreign fund inflows into local stocks and subscriptions to the almost $3.5 billion initial share sale of state-run miner \uc2 Coal India Ltd\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Trade Minister Sharma said the exchange rate movement wasn't presently volatile, a sign that the government or the Reserve Bank Of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  may not yet \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem the rise and shield Indian exporters.\par \par \uc2 "We don't see that we have reached a stage where the rupee (appreciation) can be termed as volatile," Sharma said. "The RBI and the finance ministry are keeping a close watch," Sharma added.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has reiterated it will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market only if movements in the rupee are volatile or lumpy.\par \par \uc2 -By Prasanta Sahu and Anant Vijay Kala, Dow Jones Newswires; +91-11-43563311; anant.kala@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 October 25, 2010 06:07 ET (10:07 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020101025e6ap0021k\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
