\par\par \b \uc2 RPT-INTERVIEW-India FinMin: Inflation may have hit plateau\b0\par\par\uc2 By Abhijit Neogy   \par \uc2 502 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 Oktober 2010\par 04:43\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2  (Repeats story issued on Sunday)\par \par \uc2  GYEONGJU, South Korea, Oct 24 (Reuters) - Headline inflation in India, which was at 8.62 percent in September, may have reached a plateau, Indian Finance Minister Pranab Mukherjee said.\par \par \uc2  High food prices, which have helped keep broader inflation in India well above the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's comfort zone of around 5 to 6 percent, are the result of both supply and demand factors that reflect changing consumption patterns, Mukherjee told Reuters.\par \par \uc2  The \uc2 Reserve Bank of India\uc2 , which expects headline WPI inflation to ease to 6 percent by the end of March, has raised policy rates five times this year to rein in inflation and is widely expected to raise rates by a further 25 basis points on Nov 2.\par \par \uc2  "The direction of the inflation rate movement is consistent with the Reserve Bank's projection made in the July review, though the magnitude could be slightly different. Inflation rates seem to have reached a plateau," Mukherjee said in a written response to questions from Reuters emailed late on Saturday.\par \par \uc2  India's wholesale price index (WPI) inflation was in double-digits for six months through July.\par \par \uc2  Mukherjee said food inflation, which has been stubbornly high and came in at more than 15 percent in early October on an annualised basis, is both a demand and supply issue.\par \par \uc2  "High food inflation reflects both demand and supply factors. Demand factors ... reflect changing consumption patterns," said Mukherjee, who was in South Korea for the Group of 20 meeting of finance ministers.\par \par \uc2  Rising incomes fuelled by an economy on track to grow at 8.5 percent this fiscal year have led Indians to consume more food.\par \par \uc2  IMF REFORMS\par \par \uc2  Mukherjee welcomed a G20 deal on International Monetary Fund reforms that underlines the growing clout of developing economies by giving them a bigger voice.\par \par \uc2  "Our complaint was that the quota share should reflect ground reality and economic strengths currently. Otherwise it would have eroded the credibility of the institution. That has now been corrected," Mukherjee said.\par \par \uc2  India's finance minister also said further quantitative easing by advanced economies would cause problems, and Indian policymakers will respond depending on the extent of the easing.\par \par \uc2  Emerging economies including India are seeing an influx of fund flows from the developing world as investors seek higher returns, pushing up asset prices and putting upward pressure on \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  including the rupee <INR=IN>.\par \par \uc2  The U.S. Fed is expected to embark on another round of asset purchases, which would inject more liquidity into markets. [ID:nN21128261]\par \par \uc2  Mukherjee reiterated the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's stated position that \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  only if capital flows are "lumpy" and "volatile".\par \par \uc2  Global regulators are still working on a framework for identifying systemically important financial institutions but Mukherjee said Indian banks may be be asked to maintain additional capital and liquidity levels once proposals are finalised at the G20 level.  (Editing by Tony Munroe)    \par \par \uc2 INDIA-ECONOMY/FM (INTERVIEW, REPEAT)|LANGEN|SNI   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020101028e6ap0004t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
