\par\par \b \uc2 Q+A-Will  \highlight22\uc2 India intervene \highlight\uc2  again to curb rising rupee?\b0\par\par\uc2 By Swati Bhat and Abhijit Neogy   \par \uc2 893 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 Oktober 2010\par 12:46\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 MUMBAI/NEW DELHI, Oct 19 (Reuters) - Expectations are growing that \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will continue to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets after its suspected dollar-buying last week to ease upward pressure on the rupee.\par \par \uc2 A surge of capital inflows have pushed the rupee up 6 percent since the start of September and close to 5 percent on the year, threatening to slow export growth.\par \par \uc2 The suspected dollar-buying by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which traders believe was its first such move this year, came days before \uc2 Coal India\uc2  kicked-off a $3.5 billion IPO, the country's largest, that is expected to draw billions of additional dollars into India.\par \par \uc2 More governments around the world are moving to keep their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  from appreciating or weighing capital controls as investors pour money into higher-yielding markets. [ID:nSGE69503F]\par \par \uc2 Below are some question and answers on the \uc2 Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  and \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market.\par \par \uc2 WILL RBI \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENE\b0 \highlight\uc2  AGAIN?\par \par \uc2 Possibly. The partially convertible rupee rose to a more-than two-year high beyond 44 per dollar last week.\par \par \uc2 Persistent U.S. dollar weakness and ultra-low interest rates in developed markets have prompted a surge in flows into faster-growing emerging economies that could prolong the upward pressure.\par \par \uc2 Asia's third-largest economy has attracted a record $33 billion this year from overseas into stocks and bonds, which are helpful to the extent that they can help fund a current account deficit forecast to reach $42 billion by the end of March.\par \par \uc2 More U.S. quantitative easing could unleash further flows into emerging markets like \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Exporters, which are a powerful lobby, have argued for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Margins in key industries including technology services and textiles are being squeezed by a strong rupee.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Deputy Governor Subir Gokarn said India must manage inflows so it can fund its current account deficit while at the same time not harming exports. [ID:nSGE69E03S]\par \par \uc2 Export competitiveness is more than a function of a straight two-way quote between the rupee and the dollar. The Real Effective Exchange Rate takes into account the inflation differential between two trading economies and is a better gauge of the effective exchange rate between them.\par \par \uc2 The rupee's REER based on a trade-weighed basis of six of its major trading partners provisionally moved up to 111.73 on Sept. 9, above 102.45 at the end of 2009. A level of 100 is neutral.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  maintains that it will step in to curb volatility but will not try to protect any particular level in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 HOW WILL RBI MANAGE ADDED LIQUIDITY IF IT \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENES\b0 \highlight\uc2  AGAIN?\par \par \uc2 If the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  keeps \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market it would result in more rupees entering the banking system, adding to the challenge of inflation management. Headline inflation at 8.6 percent in September is well above the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's comfort zone of 5-6 percent.\par \par \uc2 Right now, liquidity in the Indian banking system is relatively tight, due in part to funds flowing into IPO applications as well as slower-than-expected government spending of the proceeds from telecoms spectrum auctions earlier this year, making it an easier time to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The RBI can also sterilise additional rupees resulting from its dollar purchases -- something it did three years ago through the issuance of market stabilisation scheme bonds (MSS).\par \par \uc2 However, doing so is expensive given high market yields, and would add to the government's already high fiscal burden.\par \par \uc2 For 2010/11, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  fixed an upper limit of 500 billion rupees ($11.3 billion) for bond sales under MSS.\par \par \uc2 WHAT OTHER MEASURES CAN INDIA TAKE TO MANAGE INFLOWS?\par \par \uc2 Indian policymakers say they do not favour capital controls for now, but Finance Minister Pranab Mukherjee last week said he was on the lookout for "toxic assets".\par \par \uc2 India imposed controls in 2007 when it tightened rules for unregistered foreign investors by clamping down on the issuance of participatory notes, or P-notes, which were a popular vehicle for foreign investors in stocks.\par \par \uc2 RBI Governor Duvvuri Subbarao said recently that capital controls can be skirted and making frequent policy changes can send the wrong signal to investors.\par \par \uc2 India occasionally adjusts limits on flows into debt.\par \par \uc2 For example, it recently increased limits in government and corporate bonds by $5 billion each, to $10 billion and $20 billion, respectively.\par \par \uc2 The increase came with the caveat that the added limit can only be used to invest in at least 5-year paper. That encourages flows into long-term investments such as infrastructure and discourages a flood of hot money looking for short-end arbitrage.\par \par \uc2 India could also clamp down on external commercial borrowings (ECBs) by Indian companies looking to access cheaper funds from abroad, especially in a rising domestic interest rate scenario.\par \par \uc2 India could tighten the ECB tap by reducing sectoral ceilings, more stringently specifying end-uses for ECB funds and perhaps auctioning ECB entitlements. (US$1 = 44.285 rupees) (Editing by Tony Munroe) (abhijit.neogy@thomsonreuters.com; +91 11 4178 1006; Reuters Messaging: abhijit.neogy.reuters.com@reuters.net)\par \par \uc2 INDIA-ECONOMY/RUPEE (Q+A)|LANGEN|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|C|GRO|MTL|SOF|O|OIL|SNI|RNP|DNP|PCM|PEN   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020101019e6aj000qj\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
