\par\par \b \uc2 India Rupee Gains For Second Session On Inflows; Bonds Flat\b0\par\par\uc2 578 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Oktober 2010\par 15:07\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par                     As Of 1200 GMT \par                    Latest       Change \par   USD/INR           44.11       -0.41 \par   7.80% 2020 Bond   8.02%       +2 BPs \par   8.13% 2022 Bond   8.06%       -1 BPs \par   Call Rate         6.20%       -25 BPs \par   Forward Dollar Premium/Discount* \par   Aug               2.3850      +0.0875 \par  \par  *Forward Dollar Premium/Discount are midpoints of bid-offer spreads. \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 MUMBAI (Dow Jones)--The Indian rupee climbed against the U.S. dollar Thursday for the second straight session as foreign investors bought into local assets ahead of the country's largest-ever share sale next week.\f28 \par \par \uc2 The local unit was also boosted by a rally across the region after the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore said it will guide its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  higher at a slightly faster pace in a bid to contain inflation.\par \par \uc2 The dollar slipped to a fresh 25-month low against the rupee before finding heavy support from oil importers as local stocks fell.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. dollar was at INR44.11 late Thursday, lower than INR44.52 late Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 "We saw pretty heavy inflows today (Thursday), but we expect this to continue and maybe even intensify over the next two to three days," said the trading head at a foreign bank.\par \par \uc2 State-run Coal India Ltd.'s upcoming $3.5 billion initial share sale has stoked the appetite of overseas investors, who seek high returns in a fast-expanding economy. The issue, which opens Oct. 18, is expected to trigger a deluge of foreign capital inflows, which could further push the rupee up.\par \par \uc2 The bank executive expects the dollar to slip to INR43.50 in coming sessions as inflows pick up pace.\par \par \uc2 But importers continue to buy the greenback heavily at lower levels, slowing the rupee's rise, taking cues from a volatile share market wobbling amid global uncertainty.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Bombay Stock Exchange\uc2 's benchmark 30-share Sensex closed down 190.24 points, or 0.9%, at 20,497.64. The index had gained 2.4% Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 Besides, speculation on possible \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  may also limit the rupee's rise. But many participants argue that the \uc2 Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  is unlikely to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market anytime soon.\par \par \uc2 "The rupee's volatility is unlikely to last beyond a few days, so I don't see the RBI drawn away from its recent stance of avoiding \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  unless it has to," the bank executive said.\par \par \uc2 The RBI hasn't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market since November, according to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's data up to August.\par \par \uc2 In the bond market, Indian government bonds ended flat as traders avoided directional bets ahead of the key September inflation print and an INR110 billion debt sale Friday.\par \par \uc2 The benchmark 2020 bond ended at INR98.54, a tad lower than INR98.58 last close. The most-traded 8.13% 2022 bond ended at INR100.52, inching up from INR100.47 at Wednesday's close.\par \par \uc2 Traders expects the monthly wholesale price index for September to provide clarity on the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's next move at its forthcoming rate-setting meeting on Nov. 2.\par \par \uc2 India's inflation likely accelerated 8.55% from a year earlier in September, quicker than the 8.51% rise in August, according to the median estimate in a Dow Jones Newswires poll of 19 economists.\par \par \uc2 -By Khushita Vasant and Bijou George, Dow Jones Newswires; +91 22 61456115; khushita.vasant@dowjones.com [ 14-10-10 1307GMT ]\par \par \uc2 1164 1847 62506   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020101014e6ae0016d\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
