\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 India \highlight\uc2 's  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank \highlight\uc2  To  \highlight22\uc2 Intervene \highlight\uc2  In  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  Mkt If Flows Lumpy -Gov\b0\par\par\uc2 By Khushita Vasant & Subhadip Sircar   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 872 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Oktober 2010\par 15:10\par \uc2 Dow Jones News Service\par \uc2 DJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 MUMBAI (Dow Jones)--\b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market if capital inflows become volatile or lumpy, \uc2 Reserve Bank of India\uc2  Governor Duvvuri Subbarao said Saturday, in what may be a sign of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's growing concern on a sharp rupee rise. "If the inflows are lumpy and volatile or if they disrupt the macroeconomic situation, we will do so (\b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 )," Subbarao said in an address to global central bankers at the International Monetary Fund in Washington, posted on the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's website.\par \par \uc2 "Our \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  will be to keep liquidity conditions consistent with activity in the real economy and to maintain financial stability, and not to stand against developments driven by changing economic fundamentals," he said.\par \par \uc2 The comments acquire significance ahead of a host of initial share sales by companies. \uc2 Coal India Ltd\uc2 's initial public offering, which is slated to garner between $3 billion and $3.5 billion, will open on Oct. 18. It will be India's largest ever IPO.\par \par \uc2 The issue is expected to generate huge foreign fund interest and \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealers expect a sharp appreciation pressure on the rupee due to lumpy inflows in the offering.\par \par \uc2 Subbarao, however, said that the RBI has stayed away so far from the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets in spite of record portfolio inflows for a single month in September.\par \par \uc2 India has so far been swamped with capital inflows in recent months as foreign funds invest in emerging market economies seeking higher returns, resulting in the rupee's appreciation against the U.S. dollar.\par \par \uc2 Foreign funds have pumped in $5.43 billion in September alone, topping over $20 billion for 2010. The rupee has gained 3.3% in the July-September quarter.\par \par \uc2 On Thursday, the Indian rupee rose to a 25-month high against the U.S. dollar. The dollar plummeted to an intraday low of INR44.12, a level not seen since Sept. 8, 2008.\par \par \uc2 Despite a recent surge in capital inflows, India's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , unlike many of its counterparts in Asia, has stayed away from \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets.\par \par \uc2 "The reason why we did not feel the need to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  is because our absorption, driven by a widening current account deficit as imports have surged on the back of a positive outlook on growth and investment, has also increased," Subbarao said.\par \par \uc2 Japan on Friday said it will monitor the yen's ascent with "greater interest" and could foresee another \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market if deemed necessary.\par \par \uc2 Japan \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market for the first time in more than six years in September, dumping around Y2 trillion to tame the yen's upward trend, which hurt exports and the overall economy.\par \par \uc2 Several emerging market economies including Brazil and South Korea have been perturbed on the surging capital inflows ands have taken action to hold the value of their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  down.\par \par \uc2 Subbarao said the RBI has avoided flip-flops in its policy actions and that the use of controls should be prudent.\par \par \uc2 "We already do and will continue to use them (macroprudential regulations). But the operative word here is prudential."\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  recently tightened rules for bank lending against stocks.\par \par \uc2 Subbarao said that adequate \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves act as a buffer or self-insurance.\par \par \uc2 India, being a current account deficit country, the "reserves comprise essentially borrowed resources, and we are therefore more vulnerable to sudden stops and reversals as compared with countries with current account surpluses," Subbarao said.\par \par \uc2 India's April-June current account deficit widened to $13.7 billion from $4.5 billion a year ago.\par \par \uc2 Implementing Basel III A Challenge\par \par \uc2 Subbarao said growing economies such as India will face a challenge in the implementation of new global banking rules, or Basel III norms, which may require lenders to have more capital and liquidity.\par \par \uc2 That may raise lending costs for banks and reduce overall lending when credit demand begins to pick up, Subbarao said.\par \par \uc2 In July, the Basel committee put out a paper indicating the broad agreement reached on the Basel III proposals.\par \par \uc2 The proposed reforms will require banks to hold more and better-quality capital and to carry more liquid assets, which will limit their leverage and mandate them to build up capital buffers in good times that can be drawn down in periods of stress.\par \par \uc2 A Bank of International Settlements study shows that countries may have to lose about 0.2% of gross domestic product over a four-year period if the new capital regulations come into effect.\par \par \uc2 The implementations of the Basel III norms come at a time when Indian banks are witnessing loan growth. India may be the second-fastest growing economy in Asia but non-bank sources of credit and internal savings have kept bank credit growth low.\par \par \uc2 "Our credit demand will see rapid expansion because of the investment needs of infrastructure, the 'catch up' of the manufacturing sector, and most importantly, because of the credit demand that financial inclusion will bring," Subbarao said.\par \par \uc2 -By Khushita Vasant & Subhadip Sircar; Dow Jones Newswires; 91 22 6146 6145;\par \par \uc2 khushita.vasant@dowjones.com [ 10-10-10 0910ET ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJ00000020101010e6aa00008\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
