\par\par \b \uc2 =DJ MONEY TALKS: Rising Rupee Won't Force  \highlight22\uc2 India Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 By Subhadip Sircar  \par \uc2 A Dow Jones Newswires Column  \par \uc2 604 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Oktober 2010\par 09:33\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2010, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 MUMBAI (Dow Jones)--\b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is unlikely to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the near term to kneecap a sharply rising rupee.\par \par \uc2 Despite some noise from policy makers about hot-money flows, the \uc2 Reserve Bank of India\uc2  appears content to let the dollar slide against the rupee as long as the move isn't disorderly or one-sided.\par \par \uc2 That leaves India and Australia as the most prominent exceptions to the Asia-Pacific trend of central banks \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to sell their own \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in a bid to protect their exports.\par \par \uc2 RBI Deputy Gov. Subir Gokarn said Tuesday that capital inflows were emerging as a "potential threat" and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was thinking of ways to deal with it. The comments sparked off speculation the RBI may be preparing to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market for the first time since November to curb the rupee's rise.\par \par \uc2 However, there are valid reasons for the bank to stay on the sidelines.\par \par \uc2 The rupee has been a laggard in Asia for most of the year--falling 1.1% against the dollar between January and August. Recently though it has surged higher and is up around 6.7% since the beginning of September: the dollar fell to a 25-month low Thursday at INR44.12.\par \par \uc2 Foreign funds have made a beeline for Indian equities, pumping in over $20 billion so far in 2010, already exceeding all of last year's investment. Of that, $5.43 billion flowed in during September alone.\par \par \uc2 While policymakers have zeroed in on such inflows and the potential threat of asset-price bubbles down the road, \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  or any form of capital controls to rein in the rupee appears to be off the radar for now.\par \par \uc2 With the rupee's gains largely reflecting a broadly weakening dollar, \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would likely prove ineffective at this stage.\par \par \uc2 Moreover, the rupee's rise, as measured by the real effective exchange rate, has lagged those of its South Asian and North Asian export competitors in the January-August period, according to Bank of International Settlements data.\par \par \uc2 Talk of possible capital controls has also been knocked down by Finance Minister Pranab Mukherjee, who said Thursday capital inflows have not distorted market sentiment.\par \par \uc2 Inflation is another reason why the RBI may take it easy on the rupee. Consumer prices remain high, with headline inflation at 8.5% on-year in August; a higher rupee will provide some relief to policymakers as it cuts into imported inflation.\par \par \uc2 The government wouldn't want to harm foreign investor sentiment, especially at a time when it needs the inflows to bridge the current-account deficit. The April-June gap was $13.7 billion; it is forecast above 3% of gross domestic product for the fiscal year that ends March.\par \par \uc2 All of this means policy makers in India can leave the rupee alone for now.\par \par \uc2 (Subhadip Sircar is senior reporter at Dow Jones Newswires in Mumbai. He covers India's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , money and \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. He can be contacted on 91-22-61456113 or by email: subhadip.sircar@dowjones.com).