\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Rising INR Won't Force  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  - MONEY TALKS\b0\par\par\uc2 1,226 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Oktober 2010\par 09:49\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0749 GMT [Dow Jones] India's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is unlikely to step in now to cap INR's sharp rise since September. For one, INR's gains reflecting USD's slide, RBI has been of late comfortable allowing local unit to move more in line with global majors. Moreover, real effective exchange rate gains, in fact, have been less for INR vs major export competitors. Rising INR also helps in capping imported inflation and the government also doesn't seem inclined to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at this juncture, as Finance Minister Pranab Mukherjee said Thursday. Capital controls as a measure to cap INR also appear highly unlikely at a time of a yawning current account gap. See: =MONEY TALKS: Rising Rupee Won't Force \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 India Intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  (subhadip.sircar@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0749 GMT [Dow Jones] Malaysia government bonds mostly higher taking cues from U.S. Treasurys overnight; volumes low. Trader says, if U.S. Treasurys continue to rise, trend could continue next week; but players likely cautious ahead of 7-year reopening, budget both due next week. Malaysia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  plans to auction MYR3.5 billion ($1.1 billion) of September 2017 MGS Oct. 14. Yields on benchmark MGSs largely down, August 2015 down 1 bp at 3.24%, September 2017 down 1 bp at 3.55%, November 2019 flat at 3.65%. (ankur.relia@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0745 GMT [Dow Jones] Safe-haven gilts are higher as stocks slip, with traders awaiting US nonfarm payrolls at 1230 GMT. Gilts dropped Thursday as BOE kept its bond purchase program unchanged, although expectations of another round of BOE quantitative easing remain high. "This is likely to keep yields on 2-year gilts moving further below schatz and cause 10-year gilts to outperform even more relative to bunds given this is a more likely location for the BOE's purchases and therefore where the market is likely to focus its pre-emptive attention," says Richard Kelly at \uc2 TD Securities\uc2 . Ahead of payrolls, UK PPI comes at 0830 GMT. December gilts up 0.30 at 124.99, 10-year gilt up at 115.227 yielding 2.888%.(neelabh.chaturvedi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0742 GMT [Dow Jones] The moderate decline in the Swiss unemployment rate in September should continue through the rest of the year, according to Vontobel. "The adjusted rate has declined steadily since December, and as job vacancies have been buoyant recently, it should continue its downtrend," says economist Ralf Wiedenmann. (neil.maclucas@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0732 GMT [Dow Jones] Swedish industrial sentiment indicators for August due Friday are seen "solid", says SEB, and will imply "strong recovery in industrial production and new orders". Adds that expected production has been "very high" for the last 2-3 months and expectations are now at an all time high. Sweden's two-year bond yield trades 1.5bps lower at 1.40 while the 10-year also trades unchanged at 2.475.(charles.duxbury@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0732 GMT [Dow Jones] With help from strong economic numbers and surging commodity prices AUD/USD has been a star performer and is heading toward the holy grail of parity, but no further. See Nick Hastings' \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  Focus for more. (gary.stride@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0726 GMT [Dow Jones] October HSI futures down 0.3% at 22,907, while cash market hanging on to slim gains (+0.03%) as even strong A-shares market can't offset profit-taking pressure, which building up since September 1, when HSI strings up 21 sessions of gains out of 25 until Thursday. Still, contract trading at quite consistent premium to cash throughout session, suggesting view any HSI pullback due to normal profit-taking. Volume higher than recent sessions at 57,826 lots. (robert.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0724 GMT [Dow Jones] The Bank of France business survey points to a business recovery, says \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2  senior economist Dominique Barbet. Notes survey is better in terms of demand and activity across both industry and services, and capacity utilization ratio has fully corrected the August decline. Says the Bank of France's unchanged +0.3% 3Q GDP forecast in the survey is too low; \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2  expect +0.4%. (william.horobin@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0722 GMT [Dow Jones] The Polish zloty is weaker, but stays within 3.95-3.98 range against the euro. The market awaits U.S. nonfarm payrolls data later Friday, and the zloty is expected to be little changed by the time of publication, \uc2 Bank BPH\uc2  says. Adds, better than expected reading will improve global sentiment, thus PLN is likely to strengthen to below 3.95 against the euro. EUR/PLN now trades at 3.98 from 3.9639 late Thursday, USD/PLN at 2.8585 from 2.8396. (malgorzata.halaba@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0714 GMT [Dow Jones] Market expectations of the BOE announcing a further increase in asset purchases are expected to remain high ahead of the next MPC meeting in November and any such move is likely to benefit the long-end of the curve, says Steven Mansell at \uc2 Citigroup\uc2  Global Markets. "There are a number of issues in the 2034 sector and beyond where available free floats remain attractively high for a non-distortive buyback programme," he says. A lasting reduction in the fiscal risk premium should lead to flattening on the long end of the curve, which should be supported if the BOE extends its QE program. (neelabh.chaturvedi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0713 GMT [Dow Jones] KLCI down 0.1% at 1480.21 in moderate volume of 700 million shares. Local dealer says some investors staying on sidelines ahead of key U.S. jobs data, with no strong lead from regional markets to help affirm clear direction; "players are not likely to take aggressive positions ahead of the weekend but we think the general uptrend is still intact." Tips index to hover within tight 1475-1485 range for today. Advancers, decliners about equally matched. Among gainers, property plays higher on expectations of better earnings; \uc2 IJM Land\uc2  (5215.KU) +3.1% at MYR2.69, SP Setia (8664.KU) +1.0% at MYR4.95. Decliners include latex glove plays Top Glove (7113.KU), down 2.0% at MYR5.39, Kossan (7153.KU), down 1.6% at MYR3.06. Mudajaya (5085.KU) down 4.1% at MYR4.42 after company gets Securities Commission caution over inadequate disclosures. (kwan-por.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0708 GMT [Dow Jones] Mainland developers remain underperforming after Shanghai's latest tightening measures. Shimao Property (0813.HK), which probably has highest exposure to Shanghai among big developers, off 2.0% at HK$12.74. "We believe this is just the beginning -- Shanghai should have more policies to come (potentially including property tax), and more cities should come out with tightening policies," says \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2 ; continues to believe China property stocks will stay under pressure near term. \uc2 Citigroup\uc2  relatively more upbeat; while anticipates more local governments such as Hangzhou will follow in unmasking local policies, echoing central government's advocacy, "the potential impact may not be as severe as thought, especially when the tightening environment has already been in place since April this year." (robert.li@dowjones.com) [ 10-08-10 0349ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020101008e6a80006x\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw427\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal6405\pichgoal600
