\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Sensex Flat;Consolidation In Bank, Software Plays\b0\par\par\uc2 1,456 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Oktober 2010\par 10:09\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 0809 GMT [Dow Jones] Sensex remains flat, last off 0.03% at 20,537.02, in slow trade, on subdued global cues, lack of follow-on buying by funds. "It's a tepid session of trade. Banks after recent outperformance and select software shares due to rising rupee are starting to see some consolidation," says local fund manager. Adds, valuations, with Sensex trading at 19.5X this FY earnings, leave little room for further appreciation of share prices. Advises wait-and-watch approach ahead of July-September earnings due from mid-October. Expect Sensex in 20,100-20,750 range, with heavyweight \uc2 Infosys\uc2  2Q earnings, guidance due Oct. 15 to offer direction. 17 of the 30 index issues down, with HDFC (500010.BY) down 1.6% at INR733.80, \uc2 Infosys (500209.BY) \uc2 off 0.9% at INR3,075. RIL (500325.BY) +0.8% at INR1,053. Mahindra (500520.BY) down 1.4% at INR729.20, ICICI Bank (532174.BY) off 0.6% at INR1,141.95. But, \uc2 Reliance Communications (532712.BY) \uc2 +3.4% at INR182.90 on fund buying, Hindalco (500440.BY) +2.8% at INR216.25. (gurdev.singh@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0801 GMT [Dow Jones] For second time in as many days, idea of launching yuan-denominated shares in HK receiving high-profile endorsement, this time from Joseph Yam, former HKMA head. Speaking to luncheon Wednesday, Yam said he sees no reason why Beijing would object to CNY-denominated HK IPOs, would further promote CNY's internationalization while sating demand for opportunities to invest offshore yuan accumulating in HK bank accounts. HK would also benefit as would prevent volatile HKD flows common with new listings. Yam's comments echo those by HKEx CEO Charles Li yesterday at another talk. HK spearheading innovation process for yuan-based products as it positions itself as international offshore yuan center. "I would encourage the financial intermediaries to be imaginative in creating renminbi products and making better use of the renminbi for all sorts of things," he said. "Let's say using the renminbi as a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  for stock market transactions in addition to the Hong Kong dollar or whatever other \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ." See: =WSJ BLOG/China Real Time: Another Call for Yuan Shares (isabella.steger@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0800 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/MYR lower late at 3.0880 vs yesterday's 3.0910. Local dealer says trading volatile due to thin volume; pair hit intraday high of 3.1000 but profit-taking pushed pair lower in afternoon. "There are expectations Bank Negara may start selling (dollars) at MYR3.0860, like yesterday, but we don't think that level will be tested today," says dealer. Tips 3.0750-3.0950 range Friday. (kwan-por.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0759 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/JPY slides to a fresh 15-year low of 82.24 and still no sign of the BOJ. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  previously stepped in on behalf of the MOF just below 83.00 on September 15. Despite many official threats of further \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  the words have not turned into actions and the sellers are getting brave. (gary.stride@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0758 GMT [Dow Jones] iTraxx Japan Series 14 traded at 103 bps vs yesterday's 99 bps, led by widening in consumer lenders' CDS spreads, says Japan bank trader; credit protection on Promise Co. (8574.TO) done at 840 bps vs 735 bps on October 1, \uc2 Acom Co\uc2 . (8572.TO) at 550 bps vs 480 bps on Monday. Says index may widen to around 110 bps in near term, but its topside likely limited as there are no major widening factors other than concerns over consumer lending companies. Other single-name CDS deals include Japanese dollar-denominated sovereign CDS at 55 bps vs 56.5 bps Wednesday, \uc2 Bank of Tokyo-Mitsubishi\uc2  UFJ's 3-year dollar-subordinated CDS at 49, 50, dollar-subordinated protection through September 2015 at 92 bps. (megumi.fujikawa@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0757 GMT [Dow Jones] RBI hasn't approached a large state-run bank with orders to buy dollars, says senior dealer at the bank, which normally one of the banks the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  routes its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  through. USD/INR hit 25-month low of 44.12 mid-session vs 44.50 late Wednesday, but has now bounced back to near 44.20. "Not with us. I don't know if they \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  through other banks," dealer says. Adds, greenback's slide could very well be \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's reference rate related activity. "Depending on whoever has a position, they will try to push the pair (USD/INR) up or down (according to the reference rate). Somebody might have been trying to push the pair down at that time," dealer says. USD/INR reference rate set at 44.28 Thursday vs 44.30 yesterday. Says, heavy inflows seen favoring INR, which could be partly mirroring EUR rally as well as position-building ahead of \uc2 Coal India\uc2 's up to $3.5 billion share sale mid-October. "I don't expect the rupee to break the 44.00 level, but if it does, then 43.80 is a strong support as RBI may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  then." (khushita.vasant@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0756 GMT [Dow Jones] The hunt for yield could be approaching "overbought territory" says \uc2 UBS\uc2  credit strategists. "The down-in-quality trade in the high yield market is pushing CCC-rated bonds near the "overbought territory"," it says. Adds, the outperformance of CCC-rated bonds relative to BB-rated bonds is not as extreme as in mid-January and late-April, but should still be monitored closely. "The search for yields has also reduced the emerging market discount," \uc2 UBS\uc2  says. (clare.connaghan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0756 GMT [Dow Jones] Is a change in Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy on its way? \uc2 UBS\uc2  points both to Premier Kan's comment indicating that Japan wants to cooperate with G7. \uc2 UBS\uc2  also notes vice finance minister Igarashi's said that Japan does not intend to join a "\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  devaluation race." Rather \uc2 UBS\uc2  suggests Japan could conduct 'smoothing \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 .' The bank says the latter comment "does suggest a change in strategy could be in the offing, especially given the approach of Friday's G7 meeting." (nick.hastings@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0754 GMT [Dow Jones] Spanish government bond 2.50% October 2013, on auction Thursday for EUR3Bln to EUR4Bln, is cheap versus its neighbors, says \uc2 Societe Generale\uc2 , which expects to see decent demand for the paper. While the bond is cheap relative to its curve, on average 8bps cheaper than its neighbors, it is fairly valued against Italian government bonds, BTPs, SocGen says. Results are due 0830-0845 GMT. (emese.bartha@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0753 GMT [Dow Jones] While downside risks have increased for USD/INR as it tests key support at 44.15 ahead of $3 billion-$3.5 billion \uc2 Coal \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  Ltd\uc2 . IPO, \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support USD can't be ruled out, says Standard Chartered foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist Priyanka Chakravarty. "The move in the USD/INR has been rapid and, if it persists, could increase \b \highlight22\uc2 central-bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  concerns regarding volatility and competitiveness (of exports)," Chakravarty says. But in absence of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , fate of INR likely to hinge on whether capital inflows for and after \uc2 Coal India\uc2  share sale live up to expectations, as well as whether Fed moves on quantitative easing continue to pressure USD globally. USD/INR at 44.17. (bijou.george@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0750 GMT [Dow Jones] Gilts are bouncing off early lows as equities start to slip. Gilts are also getting support from weak housing data. UK house prices slumped in September by the largest monthly amount since records began in 1983, a survey by Halifax shows. While the BOE is expected to hold its policy rate and its government bond buying program unchanged (announcement at 1100 GMT), minutes due later this month could show a 3-way split in voting. Ahead of the BOE decision, UK industrial production figures are out at 0830 GMT. December gilts are down 0.07 at 124.90 vs 124.71 session low. (neelabh.chaturvedi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0750 GMT [Dow Jones] EUR/SEK may rise further Thursday if buying of euros continues on behalf of Renault (RNO.FR) following its sale of a stake in Volvo (VOLV-B.SK) worth about SEK28B says Carl Hammer at SEB. "It seems as though the bulk of the deal has passed through the market but we can't be sure there isn't more to come," says Hammer. EUR/SEK trades at 9.3244. (charles.duxbury@dowjones.com) [ 10-07-10 0409ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020101007e6a70005k\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
