\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: RBI Not Buying USD Via A State-Run Bank -Dealer\b0\par\par\uc2 1,195 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Oktober 2010\par 09:57\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 0757 GMT [Dow Jones] RBI hasn't approached a large state-run bank with orders to buy dollars, says senior dealer at the bank, which normally one of the banks the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  routes its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  through. USD/INR hit 25-month low of 44.12 mid-session vs 44.50 late Wednesday, but has now bounced back to near 44.20. "Not with us. I don't know if they \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  through other banks," dealer says. Adds, greenback's slide could very well be \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's reference rate related activity. "Depending on whoever has a position, they will try to push the pair (USD/INR) up or down (according to the reference rate). Somebody might have been trying to push the pair down at that time," dealer says. USD/INR reference rate set at 44.28 Thursday vs 44.30 yesterday. Says, heavy inflows seen favoring INR, which could be partly mirroring EUR rally as well as position-building ahead of \uc2 Coal India\uc2 's up to $3.5 billion share sale mid-October. "I don't expect the rupee to break the 44.00 level, but if it does, then 43.80 is a strong support as RBI may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  then." (khushita.vasant@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0756 GMT [Dow Jones] The hunt for yield could be approaching "overbought territory" says \uc2 UBS\uc2  credit strategists. "The down-in-quality trade in the high yield market is pushing CCC-rated bonds near the "overbought territory"," it says. Adds, the outperformance of CCC-rated bonds relative to BB-rated bonds is not as extreme as in mid-January and late-April, but should still be monitored closely. "The search for yields has also reduced the emerging market discount," \uc2 UBS\uc2  says. (clare.connaghan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0756 GMT [Dow Jones] Is a change in Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy on its way? \uc2 UBS\uc2  points both to Premier Kan's comment indicating that Japan wants to cooperate with G7. \uc2 UBS\uc2  also notes vice finance minister Igarashi's said that Japan does not intend to join a "\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  devaluation race." Rather \uc2 UBS\uc2  suggests Japan could conduct 'smoothing \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 .' The bank says the latter comment "does suggest a change in strategy could be in the offing, especially given the approach of Friday's G7 meeting." (nick.hastings@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0754 GMT [Dow Jones] Spanish government bond 2.50% October 2013, on auction Thursday for EUR3Bln to EUR4Bln, is cheap versus its neighbors, says \uc2 Societe Generale\uc2 , which expects to see decent demand for the paper. While the bond is cheap relative to its curve, on average 8bps cheaper than its neighbors, it is fairly valued against Italian government bonds, BTPs, SocGen says. Results are due 0830-0845 GMT. (emese.bartha@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0753 GMT [Dow Jones] While downside risks have increased for USD/INR as it tests key support at 44.15 ahead of $3 billion-$3.5 billion \uc2 Coal \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  Ltd\uc2 . IPO, \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support USD can't be ruled out, says Standard Chartered foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist Priyanka Chakravarty. "The move in the USD/INR has been rapid and, if it persists, could increase \b \highlight22\uc2 central-bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  concerns regarding volatility and competitiveness (of exports)," Chakravarty says. But in absence of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , fate of INR likely to hinge on whether capital inflows for and after \uc2 Coal India\uc2  share sale live up to expectations, as well as whether Fed moves on quantitative easing continue to pressure USD globally. USD/INR at 44.17. (bijou.george@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0750 GMT [Dow Jones] Gilts are bouncing off early lows as equities start to slip. Gilts are also getting support from weak housing data. UK house prices slumped in September by the largest monthly amount since records began in 1983, a survey by Halifax shows. While the BOE is expected to hold its policy rate and its government bond buying program unchanged (announcement at 1100 GMT), minutes due later this month could show a 3-way split in voting. Ahead of the BOE decision, UK industrial production figures are out at 0830 GMT. December gilts are down 0.07 at 124.90 vs 124.71 session low. (neelabh.chaturvedi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0750 GMT [Dow Jones] EUR/SEK may rise further Thursday if buying of euros continues on behalf of Renault (RNO.FR) following its sale of a stake in Volvo (VOLV-B.SK) worth about SEK28B says Carl Hammer at SEB. "It seems as though the bulk of the deal has passed through the market but we can't be sure there isn't more to come," says Hammer. EUR/SEK trades at 9.3244. (charles.duxbury@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0748 GMT [Dow Jones] Continued buying of the euro on behalf of Renault (RNO.FR) after its Volvo (VOLV-B.SK) stake sale could drive EUR/SEK higher Thursday, says \uc2 Citigroup\uc2 . "\uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  was the sole book runner on the deal and was seen in the market hoovering EUR/SEK," says \uc2 Citigroup\uc2 , adding "players will looking for clues that it has finished". EUR/SEK trades at a 1-month high of 9.3337.(charles.duxbury@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0739 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/JPY is trading at a fresh 15-year low and bang on the 2-year support line at 82.62 notes \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  analyst Karen Jones. She looks for this to hold and provoke a recovery toward the 20-day MA at 84.15 and the 55-day MA at 84.98. Alternatively, a sustained break below 82.62 opens risk toward 80.00. USD/JPY now at 0.8263. (gary.stride@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0737 GMT [Dow Jones] Malaysia government bonds mostly tad higher following overnight rally in U.S. Treasurys. Local dealer says volume thin, market players staying on sidelines ahead of reopening of 7-year benchmark MGS, August trade data both due tomorrow. Adds, yields also likely to stay rangebound next week ahead of budget due Friday. Yields for November 2019 MGS down 1 bp at 3.64%, September 2017 MGS flat at 3.56%, August 2015 MGS down 2 bps at 3.24%. (kwan-por.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0737 GMT [Dow Jones] French August foreign trade figures point to robust economic activity in August and to very dynamic 3Q trade, says \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2  senior economist Dominique Barbet. 3Q exports and imports would both gain 7.5% on the quarter if export and import levels remain unchanged in September, which is a conservative hypothesis, says Barbet. (william.horobin@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0736 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/HKD stays in tight range, amid absence of leads with China markets closed a week for National Day holiday, only resuming activity tomorrow, says trader at Singapore bank; tips pair in 7.7540-7.7570 band near-term. "We see sluggish stock moves (HSI down 0.1%), and China markets still closed for today, so there's nothing in particular to draw investors' attention." Pair last at 7.7559, vs 7.7561 in morning, 7.7552 yesterday. (susanna.tai@dowjones.com) [ 10-07-10 0357ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020101007e6a70005e\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
