\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: India Call Rates, CBLO Up; Liquidity Still Tight\b0\par\par\uc2 1,680 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 September 2010\par 07:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0500 GMT [Dow Jones] India overnight call rates higher as liquidity in system still tight, says dealer with state-run bank; interbank call rate on RBI's negotiated dealing system at 6.20% vs 5.90% at Tuesday's close, collateralized borrowing and lending obligation or CBLO at 5.97%, closer to repo rate of 6.00%. "Liquidity continues to remain a constraint and overnight rates will hover at or above the repo rate," says dealer. Tips call rates in 6.00%-6.20% range, CBLO in 5.50%-6.00% in session. Notes, banks likely to borrow INR400 billion at RBI's liquidity auction in day. (nupur.acharya@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0457 GMT [Dow Jones] NZ shares flat at 3230.17, though some heavyweights lower on profit-taking with Fletcher Building (FBU.NZ) down 0.7% at NZ$8.60, Telecom (TEL.NZ) down 1.9% at NZ$2.02, says Hamilton Hindin Greene broker Grant Williamson. Notes Pumpkin Patch (PPL.NZ) flat at NZ$1.95 after reporting in-line FY results. Guinness Peat (GPG.NZ) +1.5% at NZ$0.67 as investors remain upbeat about appointment of 4 new independent directors. But overall, "it's a relatively quiet day. There is not a lot of turnover, just a little bit of profit-taking and no clear direction." Says investors will remain focused on offshore markets for cues. While tough to predict how markets may go later in global trading day; "you would have to say the trend is certainly positive at the moment." (rebecca.howard@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0456 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/MYR extends fall to fresh 13-year low of 3.0910 vs 3.1000 late Tuesday; two dealers say \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  spotted buying dollars at 3.0910. "Bank Negara is buying in small doses to smoothen the flow...volume is a bit thinner today compared with yesterday," says one dealer. Tips immediate support at 3.0900; then 3.0750 (October 17, 1997 low) in medium term. (kwan-por.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0455 GMT [Dow Jones] Nikkei 225 December futures flat at 9530 amid relatively low participation levels as China markets are closed, Japan markets close tomorrow for holiday; while USD/JPY hovering below 85, investors stay alert that government may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market (again). "Investors are worried about the possibility that there may be major moves in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market and in overseas markets tomorrow when Japanese markets are not trading," says Hiroyuki Fukunaga, CEO of Investrust, adding that both futures and cash market may end near flat today. "Investors don't want to take positions." (ayai.tomisawa@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0450 GMT [Dow Jones] NZD/USD firming as USD broadly weaker following Fed announcement this morning, says RBS \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist Greg Gibbs; "The market is clearly looking to buy higher yielding \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  against the U.S. dollar and the Kiwi is benefiting from that." Pair last 0.7366. Adds NZ 2Q Current Account deficit mostly ignored by the market; "I guess it is primarily about U.S. weakness at the moment and you just need to have the data not be too alarming one way or the other and I think the Kiwi can continue to firm." Says overnight market watching euro zone industrial orders, with U.S. durable goods and housing figures due out later in week. Tips support at today's low of 0.7320 with short term resistance at 0.7400, further resistance at 0.7440. (lucy.craymer@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0449 GMT [Dow Jones] \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is latest buzz in Asia's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets; \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  remains an outlier, says Heard on the Street. Not for lack of volatility: INR has jumped 3.4% vs USD so far in September -- most of that in last week alone; steep gain has \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders looking for signs RBI might step in. There's good reason, though, to think it won't. For one, \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  will add capital to \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 's banking system, undermining RBI's fight against inflation. True, it can offset additional liquidity by sterilization, but not without a cost. Recent rate hikes in India mean difference in yields between two-year Indian bonds, U.S. Treasurys has widened to 587 bps from 350 bps eight months ago, \uc2 Royal Bank of Scotland\uc2  says; creates negative carry for RBI. Anyway, over long-term, India's widening current account deficit, takes care of this job. Unlike for its peers in the region, \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is one task off RBI's desk. See: =HEARD ON THE STREET: India Unruffled By The Rupee's Swing (harsh.joshi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0448 GMT [Dow Jones]BOJ offers to inject total of Y1.4 trillion into money market at 0400 GMT via both fixed-rate and auction-style \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  offers to supply ample amount of funds, with about Y2 trillion fund shortage expected for Friday (Japan markets will be closed Thursday for national holiday), analysts say. BOJ forecast in morning \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's current account balance to be around Y18.2 trillion. (andrew.monahan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0441 GMT [Dow Jones] Singapore shares shrug off initial fatigue, with interest returning even to blue chips, which have b