nding to follow rate moves in U.S. due to \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  peg to greenback. Cheung Kong (0001.HK) +2.3% at HK$111.70, \uc2 Sun Hung Kai Properties\uc2  (0016.HK) +1.9% at HK$125.30, \uc2 Sino Land\uc2  (0083.HK) +2.0% at HK$15.70. "I think the index could test its April high (22,388) in the near-term, as continued weakness in the dollar is expected to propel further gains in the equity markets." Volume brisk at HK$40.28 billion. (susanna.tai@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0438 GMT [Dow Jones] KLCI +0.1% at 1477.24 midday after choppy, range-bound morning session; key index swung between positive, negative territories, but confined to narrow 4-point range. Market breadth neutral with number of decliners, advancers about even. "The market is merely in consolidation mode at the moment due to a lack of leads. The uptrend is certainly not yet over," dealer says; tips KLCI to remain in 1470-1480 range near-term. Technical analysts say only fall below 1450 would signal upward momentum slowing. Builders deemed as potential beneficiaries of major projects being considered under government's 10-year economic plan in focus today; \uc2 YTL Corp\uc2  (4677.KU) +2.9% at MYR7.81, Gamuda (5398.KU) +5.1% at MYR3.94, MMC (2194.KU) +4.7% at MYR2.91. Among decliners, glovemakers extend losses on continued concerns over slowing demand growth. Top Glove (7113.KU) down 2.9% at MYR5.39. (benjamin.low@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0438 GMT [Dow Jones] Philippine shares end lower, weighed by profit-taking on major blue chips; benchmark PSEi closes down 0.8% at 4054.18 in heavy volume, though market breadth still positive, with gainers edging out losers 83 to 76; "The market is just taking a breather...but I think the market really needs to correct before resuming its advance," says Ron Rodrigo, research head at DBP-Daiwa Securities; support pegged at 4000, resistance 4100. Decliners led by conglomerate SM Investments (SM.PH), down 2.9% at PHP510, property developer