\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: India Unruffled By INR Swing -HEARD ON THE STREET\b0\par\par\uc2 1,832 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 September 2010\par 06:49\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 0449 GMT [Dow Jones] \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is latest buzz in Asia's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets; \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  remains an outlier, says Heard on the Street. Not for lack of volatility: INR has jumped 3.4% vs USD so far in September -- most of that in last week alone; steep gain has \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders looking for signs RBI might step in. There's good reason, though, to think it won't. For one, \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  will add capital to \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 's banking system, undermining RBI's fight against inflation. True, it can offset additional liquidity by sterilization, but not without a cost. Recent rate hikes in India mean difference in yields between two-year Indian bonds, U.S. Treasurys has widened to 587 bps from 350 bps eight months ago, \uc2 Royal Bank of Scotland\uc2  says; creates negative carry for RBI. Anyway, over long-term, India's widening current account deficit, takes care of this job. Unlike for its peers in the region, \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is one task off RBI's desk. See: =HEARD ON THE STREET: India Unruffled By The Rupee's Swing (harsh.joshi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0448 GMT [Dow Jones]BOJ offers to inject total of Y1.4 trillion into money market at 0400 GMT via both fixed-rate and auction-style \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  offers to supply ample amount of funds, with about Y2 trillion fund shortage expected for Friday (Japan markets will be closed Thursday for national holiday), analysts say. BOJ forecast in morning \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's current account balance to be around Y18.2 trillion. (andrew.monahan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0441 GMT [Dow Jones] Singapore shares shrug off initial fatigue, with interest returning even to blue chips, which have been underperforming lower liners lately. STI +0.6% at 3114.02 midday vs down 0.3% at 3084.89 earlier. Lower liners stronger, with FTSE ST Small Cap Index +1.2%, FTSE ST Mid Cap Index +1.1%. Overall volume robust at 1.98 billion shares worth S$1.39 billion. "Due to the curbs by the government on property investments, money and capital is being diverted to the stock market," says trader; adds, "stay invested. A rising tide will lift all boats." Singapore government last month unveiled third round of housing-market curbs in 12 months in bid to rein in property speculation. Key gainers among blue chips include NOL (N03.SG), +3.5% at S$2.07, \uc2 CapitaLand\uc2  (C31.SG), +2.8% at S$4.09, SGX (S68.SG), +2.4% at S$8.70. Among lower liners, Cosco (F83.SG) +5.2% at S$1.82, Biosensors (B20.SG) +5.2% at S$1.02, CSC (C06.SG) +9.1% at S$0.18. (frankie.ho@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0439 GMT [Dow Jones] Vietnam shares end +0.1% at 453.9 vs day's high 457.0, on 3Q earnings expectations. But many players still hesitant to enter market, wait to see what new measures to boost cash supply government will introduce; government to hold major meeting to review economic performance for the quarter at end of month. "Everyone has faith that the government will have to introduce stronger measures after it last week raised GDP growth target set for this year to +6.7% vs +6.5%. Also among the possible measures, it is likely to cut interest rates," says analyst. Volume thin at 33.4 million shares valued at VND908 billion exchanging hands. Among most active, Ocean Group (OGC.VH) ends down 4.8% at VND35,800, Eximbank (EIB.VH) ends flat at VND17,600, \uc2 Quoc Cuong Gia Lai\uc2  (QCG.VH) end +4.7% at VND31,300. Tips more gains tomorrow with key index to move in 455-460 range. (phammuoi.nguyen@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0439 GMT [Dow Jones] HSI +0.8% at 22,169.93 midday, supported by gains in HK property stocks after Fed statement indicates interest rates to remain low for while, with HK banks tending to follow rate moves in U.S. due to \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  peg to greenback. Cheung Kong (0001.HK) +2.3% at HK$111.70, \uc2 Sun Hung Kai Properties\uc2  (0016.HK) +1.9% at HK$125.30, \uc2 Sino Land\uc2  (0083.HK) +2.0% at HK$15.70. "I think the index could test its April high (22,388) in the near-term, as continued weakness in the dollar is expected to propel further gains in the equity markets." Volume brisk at HK$40.28 billion. (susanna.tai@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0438 GMT [Dow Jones] KLCI +0.1% at 1477.24 midday after choppy, range-bound morning session; key index swung between positive, negative territories, but confined to narrow 4-point range. Market breadth neutral with number of decliners, advancers about even. "The market is merely in consolidation mode at the moment due to a lack of leads. The uptrend is certainly not yet over," dealer says; tips KLCI to remain in 1470-1480 range near-term. Technical analysts say only fall below 1450 would signal upward momentum slowing. Builders deemed as potential beneficiaries of major projects being considered under government's 10-year economic plan in focus today; \uc2 YTL Corp\uc2  (4677.KU) +2.9% at MYR7.81, Gamuda (5398.KU) +5.1% at MYR3.94, MMC (2194.KU) +4.7% at MYR2.91. Among decliners, glovemakers extend losses on continued concerns over slowing demand growth. Top Glove (7113.KU) down 2.9% at MYR5.39. (benjamin.low@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0438 GMT [Dow Jones] Philippine shares end lower, weighed by profit-taking on major blue chips; benchmark PSEi closes down 0.8% at 4054.18 in heavy volume, though market breadth still positive, with gainers edging out losers 83 to 76; "The market is just taking a breather...but I think the market really needs to correct before resuming its advance," says Ron Rodrigo, research head at DBP-Daiwa Securities; support pegged at 4000, resistance 4100. Decliners led by conglomerate SM Investments (SM.PH), down 2.9% at PHP510, property developer Megaworld (MEG.PH), down 2.4% at PHP2.40 and PLDT (TEL.PH), down 0.3% at PHP2,596 -- all gainers in recent days; gainers were led by Philippine National Bank (PNB.PH), +6.2% at PHP60 while gold-copper producer \uc2 Philex Mining\uc2  (PX.PH), +3.1% at PHP15.98. (cris.larano@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0438 GMT [Dow Jones] Nikkei off 0.2% at 9585.74; trading cues seen lacking with Chinese and South Korean markets closed for public holiday; investors also cautious about yen movement while Japan markets close tomorrow for holiday. "Investors want to confirm (fiscal) first-half corporate earnings and the second-half outlook before bidding shares higher," says Masatoshi Sato, senior strategist at Mizuho Investors Securities. "It is difficult to draw a scenario for the Nikkei to top 10,000 with USD/JPY hovering at its current level (84-85)." Adds, worries over deterioration in China-Japan ties over shipping incident also weighing on market. 14/33 Topix subindexes lower; Komatsu (6301.TO) down 1.8% at Y1,888, \uc2 Sony\uc2  (6758.TO) down 1.1% at Y2,608. Real estate sector top performer (up 1.2%) on signs decline in residential land prices for Japan's major cities may be bottoming out; \uc2 Sumitomo Realty & Development\uc2  (8830.TO) up 1.6% at Y1,720. (kana.inagaki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0434 GMT [Dow Jones] India sovereign bonds up on cues from overnight rally in U.S. Treasurys but tight liquidity may cap large gains, says dealer with state-run banks; 7.80% 2020 benchmark bond at 99.07 vs 98.92 at last close. "Any large movements ahead of RBI's INR110 billion auction is unlikely, though the borrowing calendar for October-March will be a major cue on how markets will position itself," says dealer. Government, RBI will meet to finalize borrowing calendar for October-March period on Thursday. RBI to hold its last auction, for fiscal 1H ending September, of three government securities including 10-year benchmark for INR40 billion on Friday. Dealer tips 2020 bond yields in 7.90%-7.97% range in session, last 7.94%. (nupur.acharya@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0434 GMT [Dow Jones] Fed's dovish statement overnight bolstering bullish sentiment for precious metals; gold, silver, platinum all edging towards recent highs. BMO Capital Markets analyst Bart Melek says "considerable risks associated with the euro and the U.S. dollar over the long term (revaluation of the Chinese yuan and massive fiscal imbalances), growing interest in commodities as a bona fide asset class and a hedge, and producer de-hedging prompt us to see gold and the precious metals complex in a very positive light well into 2011." Spot gold last at $1,288.60/oz, +$1.80 since NY close, just off all-time record nominal high of $1,290.55; spot silver at $20.96, +1 cent, just off overnight 31-month high of $21.06. Spot platinum at $1,629/oz, +$10, highest level since May 19. "Rising cost of production, especially in South Africa after (power producer) Eskom's sharp price increases, should provide a bullish case for these metals (platinum and palladium), as will growing structural deficits," he says. Spot palladium last at $536/oz, +$3. (james.campbell@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0432 GMT [Dow Jones] (An item at 0343 GMT misstated the type of USD/JPY option a trader saw being bought. The correct version follows:) Volatilities implied by 1-month ATM USD/JPY options unchanged at 10.50%/11.20% from New York Tuesday. Options dealer at major Japanese bank says though spot USD/JPY falls below 85.00, demand for downside protection remains low as investors expect Japan authorities will again \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  if there is significant further fall, following \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  last week at low of 82.87. Says vols may stay around current levels unless spot falls below 84.50 (currently 84.88). If USD/JPY does break that level, vols may rise to 11.00%, dealer says. Single player buys 1-week USD-call/JPY-put contracts at strike price of 85.00 with implied vols of 10.55% for about $100 million, same dealer says. (kosaku.narioka@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0420 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/IDR finds strong support at 8,950; Bank Indonesia suspected to have bought around $200 million near 8,950-8,960 to stem IDR's rise, 3 dealers say. Pair last at 8,955 vs 8,965 late yesterday. "Bank Indonesia is supporting the dollar at around IDR8,950" but \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  probably won't lift pair higher because greenback is weak worldwide, dealer with foreign bank says. 8,950-8,970 range tipped for rest of day. (i-made.sentana@dowjones.com) [ 09-22-10 0049ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100922e69m0002r\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
