\par\par \b \uc2 HEARD ON THE STREET:  \highlight22\uc2 India \highlight\uc2  Unruffled By Rupee Swing\b0\par\par\uc2 By Harsh Joshi   \par \uc2 A DOW JONES COLUMN   \par \uc2 491 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 September 2010\par 01:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is the latest buzz in Asia's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  remains an outlier.\par \par \uc2 It's not for a lack of volatility in the rupee: The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has jumped 3.4% against the greenback so far in September -- most of that in the last week alone. The gains have come during a surge in investment into India's capital markets. Foreigners have bought $3 billion worth of Indian shares already in September alone , nearly a fifth of their total buying for the year so far.\par \par \uc2 The steep gain has \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders looking for signs the \uc2 Reserve Bank of India\uc2  might step in and slow things down. There's good reason, though, to think it won't.\par \par \uc2 For one, \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  against a rising rupee will add capital to \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 's banking system -- undermining the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's own fight against inflation. The inflation problem has forced the RBI to raise banks' reserve requirements, for example, to ensure that they have less to lend.\par \par \uc2 True, the RBI can offset the additional liquidity by selling so-called sterilization bonds to Indian banks -- again with the intention of tying their capital up, but that's not without a cost. India typically invests its reserves in U.S. Treasurys, where yields are lower than what the RBI will pay those banks, creating a situation called negative carry in which the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  loses money as those sterilization bonds stack up. Recent rate hikes in India mean the difference in yields between two-year Indian bonds and U.S. Treasurys has widened to 5.87 percentage points from 3.5 percentage points eight months ago, the \uc2 Royal Bank of Scotland\uc2  says.\par \par \uc2 Anyway, over the long-term, India's economic fundamentals take care of the RBI's job here. India imports more than it exports, and as the country's importers busily convert their rupees into dollars they also offset the inflow of global investor funds. Widening current account deficit, meanwhile, is a good reason for the RBI to stay away from blocking the inflow of dollars.\par \par \uc2 Unlike for its peers in the region, \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is one task off the RBI's desk.\par \par \uc2 (Harsh Joshi is a Heard on the Street columnist in Mumbai. He has been with Dow Jones Newswires since 2007 and covered the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , money and \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, and the banking sector. He can be reached at harsh.joshi@dowjones.com or at +91-22-6145-6101. )\par \par \uc2 TALK BACK: We invite readers to send us comments on this or other financial news topics. Please email us at TalkbackAsia@dowjones.com. Readers should include their full names, work or home addresses and telephone numbers for verification purposes. We reserve the right to edit and publish your comments along with your name; we reserve the right not to publish reader comments. [ 21-09-10 2300GMT ]\par \par \uc2 5090   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020100921e69l001cw\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
