\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-No MSS bond issuance in near term-India finmin source\b0\par\par\uc2 430 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 April 2010\par 09:58\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (Adds details)\par \par \uc2 NEW DELHI, April 21 (Reuters) - India's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may not issue market stabilisation scheme (MSS) bonds in the near term as there is no excess liquidity in the market, a senior finance ministry official said on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 "There is sufficient liquidity in the market. But we can't say that there is excessive liquidity," the official, who declined to be named, told Reuters.\par \par \uc2 A strengthening rupee on foreign inflows has fuelled market speculation that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may start issuing the market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  bonds.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  can issue \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  bonds of upto 500 billion rupees ($11.3 billion) in the 2010/11 fiscal year that started on April 1.\par \par \uc2 Earlier on Wednesday, \uc2 Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  (RBI) \uc2 Governor Duvvuri Subbarao said issuance of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  bonds would be more difficult in the current fiscal year given the low market appetite. [ID:nBMA007382]\par \par \uc2 The Indian rupee, which has gained about 4.7 percent in 2010, rose to a 19-month peak this month on foreign fund inflows into the equity market. Foreigners have bought shares of about $5.7 billion so far in 2010.\par \par \uc2 A Reuters poll conducted last week showed the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is expected to appreciate about 1.2 percent against the dollar between now and end-December.[ID:nSGE63E01W]\par \par \uc2 The official said the finance ministry and the RBI are yet to decide on the timing and the maturity date of the new 10-year benchmark bond.\par \par \uc2 Early this month, sources had told Reuters that the issuance of a new 10-year benchmark bond could be delayed as the government and the RBI want to increase volumes in the existing benchmark paper and avoid volatility. [ID:nSGE6360EA]\par \par \uc2 Traders in the bond market were expecting the government to sell a new 10-year bond in the first week of this month, but the bond on offer, instead, was the current benchmark -- the 6.35 percent 2020 maturity bond.\par \par \uc2 Traders had been concerned the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may issue a fresh 10-year bond, which would have pushed them to sell the 6.35 percent 2020 bond in order to make room for the new issue.\par \par \uc2 The official also said the government has no plans currently to issue cash management bills (CMBs). The government uses CMBs to meet temporary mismatches in its finances. ($1=44.42 Indian rupees)\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Rajesh Kumar Singh and Manoj Kumar; editing by Malini Menon)\par \par \uc2 INDIA-ECONOMY/FINMIN (UPDATE 1)|LANGEN|SNI|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|RNP|DNP   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020100421e64l000ho\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
