\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Sensex Likely Tad Higher, 17,850-18,050 Tipped\b0\par\par\uc2 1,600 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 April 2010\par 05:25\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 0325 GMT [Dow Jones] Sensex called to open tad higher, by about 20-30 points, aided by positive trade in some Asian markets and as sentiment remains upbeat amid sustained buying interest by foreign funds, says local fund manager. Adds, ample liquidity and buying by traders on expectations of good January-March quarter earnings from next week could further bolster index. Likes automobile and metal plays on hopes of strong earnings growth and technology stocks after recent underperformance. Foreign funds purchased INR27.16 billion worth shares over previous three sessions. Still, reckons Sensex could face resistance at 18,250 near term. Says, most index blue chips' valuations stretched after rising more than 5% over past month, recent outperformance vs most global equities could lead to some consolidation this week. Tips Sensex in 17,850-18,050 range vs ending flat at 17,941.37 last session. (gurdev.singh@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0322 GMT [Dow Jones] Thai shares +1.1% at 816.82, propelled by continuous capital inflows, gains on most major regional bourses. Energy plays lead gains as crude oil price rises. But further upside likely limited by mounting political tensions after anti-government demonstrators occupy Bangkok's main business area for 5th day, says Bualuang Securities executive vice president Chaiyaporn Nompitakcharoen. "Foreign investors' exposure in the Thai stock market remains minimal, while valuation remains cheaper than other regional bourses, so fund inflows are expected to continue if protests remain peaceful," he adds. Resistance tipped at 820, support at 800. Among most active, PTT (PTT.TH) +2.9% at THB281, PTT Chemical (PTTCH.TH) +1.8% at THB114, \uc2 PTT Exploration & Production\uc2  (PTTEP.TH) +1.6% at THB154, Banpu (BANPU.TH) +1.6% at THB644, \uc2 Krung Thai Bank\uc2  (KTB.TH) +3.1% at THB13.20. (oranan.paweewun@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0315 GMT [Dow Jones] It must be some law of finance akin to Murphy's Law that says whenever officials aren't predicted to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, they do. Sure enough, Dow Jones reports RBI suspected of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  Tuesday morning, buying USD/INR at 44.35. Traders and analysts say suspected moves likely due to either desire to slow (but not reverse) INR's rise, or as warning to those tempted to think INR's movements will only be one way - and plan trading strategies accordingly. "The RBI was probably a bit uncomfortable with the speed of the rally, so it probably moved in to smooth things out a bit," one trader with foreign bank told Dow Jones. In other words, RBI's actions mostly aimed at smoothing USD/INR volatility, not pegging INR to any specific level. See: =WSJ BLOG/\b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  Real Time: The Meaning of \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  (abhrajit.gangopadhyay@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0314 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/INR likely to fall further to fresh 19-month low on stronger Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , expected positive local stocks, says dealer with private bank; pair likely in 44.25-44.50 band vs 44.46 late yesterday. "It seems like another day in favour of the rupee though demand from oil companies may restrict the fall in pair." Notes 44.23 strong support, though difficult for pair to go below 44.30 unless strong capital inflows come in play. Foreign funds net buyers of provisional INR2.62 billion worth of Indian stocks yesterday. 1-month NDFs quoting at 44.45/44.49. (nupur.acharya@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0312 GMT [Dow Jones] BOJ policy board decides unanimously to keep policy interest-rate target on hold at 0.1%, as it gauges effectiveness of previous easing measures, including doubling of supply of 0.1% 3-month loan facility to Y20 trillion in March, as financial markets stay calm, some positive signals for economy. BOJ also maintains assessment of overall economy, saying "Japan's economy has been picking up." All eyes now on Governor Shirakawa's comments at press conference starting from 0630 GMT, market watching for hints at possibility of further easing. (megumi.fujikawa@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0309 GMT [Dow Jones] Indonesia shares +0.5% 2894.155 in light volume, helped by firmer IDR, gains in many Asian markets; resistance remains at 2900. "The main index has been moving in a very limited range to reflect caution over whether profit-taking will take place after the recent rally," says trader at Reliance Securities. Among gainers: BCA (BBCA.JK) +2.6% at IDR5,950, \uc2 Indosat\uc2  (ISAT.JK) +1.7% at IDR6,100, while cigarette maker \uc2 Gudang Garam\uc2  (GGRM.JK) +2.3% at IDR26,200. Profit-taking hits coal miner Bumi (BUMI.JK), down 2.0% at IDR2,500 after surging 11% yesterday. (edhi.pranasidhi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0307 GMT [Dow Jones] \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2  expects Singapore SGD NEER policy to switch to "modest and gradual appreciation" possibly later this month vs neutral previously; thinks NEER band slope likely to return to 2.25%, so to have "greater monetary flexibility to address growth or inflation concerns as they arise in the future." Says Singapore's inflation still low, but global economic data have been strong over last several weeks; "but given continuing concern, even if reduced, about the medium term economic outlook and because MAS moves tend not to be large, we would expect the MAS to choose the more conservative slope." Adds, SGD NEER now trading at over 100 bps on strong side of band, "suspected" MAS buying also caps SGD strength. (aries.poon@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0305 GMT [Dow Jones] Philippine shares extending gains, helped by a steady stream of foreign funds accumulating major blue chips; benchmark PSEi at fresh 26-month high of 3274.24, +0.6% in moderate volume; support 3200, resistance 3300. Jomar Lacson, research head at Campos, Lanuza & Co., notes an increase in foreign funds inflow into blue chips, an indication foreign funds are realigning portfolio as U.S. interest rates appear likely to stay low for a while. "At the rate shares prices are climbing, however, valuations are making them unattractive," says Lacson, making them susceptible to profit-taking. Property stocks leading gainers: Ayala Land (ALI.PH) +3.8% at PHP13.75, Megaworld (MEG.PH) +4.7% at PHP1.34, and Filinvest Land (FLI.PH) +4.3% at PHP0.97, which are expected to return stronger sales amid low interest rates and expected economic recovery. (cris.larano@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0304 GMT [Dow Jones] Vietnam shares last +0.3% at 517.12, on investor buying after government requested \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  earlier today to lower interest rates gradually. "Sentiment is improving because it seems the authorities have realized that they have to act, because many businesses have been forced to pay interest rates as high as 19% a year for months, and company profitability has been hurt seriously," says Hanoi-based trader; tips key index in 514-518 range today. Volume moderate at 24 million shares, valued at VND985 billion; Hoa Phat Group (HPG.VH) +0.7% at VND67,500, \uc2 PetroVietnam Finance\uc2  (PVF.VH) +0.7% at VND29,700, Phuoc Hoa Rubber (PHR.VH) +4.6% at VND43,500. (phammuoi.nguyen@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0300 GMT [Dow Jones] While cut by Australia's AWB (AWB.AU) to its estimated returns on collective, or pool, sales of 2009-10 crop year wheat likely expected, given soft global prices, move will add to generally bearish local sentiment. AWB's General Manager Australian Commodities Mitch Morison tells of large global stocks, softening prices, plus "many buyers are standing aside, waiting for the market to consolidate before committing to new purchases." Nearby ASX milling wheat futures deliverable May in NSW lost A$6.00 yesterday to settle at A$192.50/ton, down from A$245 on December 1. Plentiful rain this year in eastern Australia would normally have growers eyeing big planting programs, but many will curb plans given low prices. (ray.brindal@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0259 GMT [Dow Jones] India government bonds likely to trade in range but biased down ahead of RBI's INR120 billion bond auction due Friday, which will likely decide near-term market direction, says dealer; 6.35% 2020 benchmark bond yield eyed in 7.77%-7.85% range vs 7.80% (90.23 in price terms) Tuesday's close. "The short positions would emerge as market gets ready for the first auction of the new fiscal year. Also, high US Treasury yields may weigh on the sentiments," says dealer with state-run bank. (nupur.acharya@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0243 GMT [Dow Jones] Korea treasury bonds, bond futures moderately higher, tracking gains in U.S. Treasurys overnight, analysts say; 3-year yield down 2 bps at 3.82%, 5-year yield down 5 bps at 4.44%, June futures +9 ticks at 110.79. Government will likely buy back KRW2 trillion in treasuries this year, using part of budget surplus generated last year, finance ministry says Wednesday. "This would further help improve the market's supply and demand conditions," says \uc2 Daewoo Securities\uc2  analyst Yoon Il-kwang. Still, IBK Securities analyst Ough Chang-sup doesn't expect sharp moves ahead of BOK's rate-setting monetary policy meeting Friday, puts 3-year yield support at 3.80%. Majority of bond-market participants expect BOK to leave its key policy rate unchanged Friday; prevailing market view that any rate hike is unlikely in 1H, Korea Financial Investment Association says. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com) [ 04-06-10 2325ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100407e6470002d\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
