\par\par \b \uc2 Reserve Bank of India\uc2  - DRG Study on \u8216\'20\'18Exchange Rate Policy and Modelling in India\u8217\'20\'19\b0\par\par\uc2 952 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 Februar 2010\par \uc2 ENP Newswire\par \uc2 ENPNEW\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010, Electronic News Publishing. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Reserve Bank of India\uc2  released a DRG Study entitled, \u8216\'20\'18Exchange Rate Policy and Modelling in India\u8217\'20\'19.\par \par \uc2 The Study is co-authored by Prof. Pami Dua, Professor in the Department of Economics of Delhi School of Economics along with Dr. Rajiv Ranjan, Director in the Department of Economic Analysis and Policy of the \uc2 Reserve Bank of India\uc2 . The study covers two main topics: first, various aspects of economic policy with respect to the exchange rate and second, modelling and forecasting the exchange rate.\par \par \uc2 The study analyses India\u8217\'20\'19s exchange rate policy and discusses the structure of the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market in India in terms of participants, instruments and trading platform, as also, turnover in the Indian \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market and forward premia. The study then attempts to develop a model for the rupee-dollar exchange rate taking into account variables from monetary and micro structure models as well as other variables including \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The focus is on the exchange rate of the Indian rupee vis-\u224\'00\'E0-vis the US dollar, i.e., the Re/$ rate.\par \par \uc2 The study also covers the exchange rate policy of India in the background of large capital flows. The movement of the Indian rupee is largely influenced by the capital flow movements rather than traditional determinants like trade flows. Though capital flows are generally seen to be beneficial to an economy, a large surge in flows over a short span of time in excess of the domestic absorptive capacity can be a source of stress to the economy giving rise to upward pressures on the exchange rate, overheating of the economy, and possible asset price bubbles.\par \par \uc2 The study notes that an important aspect of the policy response in \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  to the various episodes of volatility has been market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  combined with monetary and administrative measures to meet the threats to financial stability while complementary or parallel recourse has been taken to communication through speeches and press releases. In line with the exchange rate policy, it has also been observed that the Indian rupee is moving along with the economic fundamentals in the post-reform period. Moving forward, as India progresses towards full capital account convertibility and gets more and more integrated with the rest of the world, managing periods of volatility is bound to pose greater challenges in view of the impossible trinity of independent monetary policy, open capital account and exchange rate management. Preserving stability in the market would require more flexibility, adaptability and innovations with regard to the strategy for liquidity management as well as exchange rate management. With the likely turnover in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market rising in future, further development of the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market will be crucial to manage the associated risks.\par \par \uc2 After studying various aspects of economic policy with respect to the exchange rate, the study attempts to gauge the forecasting ability of economic models with respect to exchange rates with the difference that this is done in the context of a developing country that follows a managed floating (as opposed to flexible) exchange rate regime. Starting from the na\u239\'00\'EFve model, the study examines the forecasting performance of the monetary model and various extensions of it in the vector autoregressive (VAR) and Bayesian vector autoregressive (BVAR) framework. The study examines, first, whether the monetary model can beat a random walk. Second, it investigates if the forecasting performance of the monetary model can be improved by extending it. Third, the study evaluates the forecasting performance of a VAR model vs a BVAR model. Lastly, it considers if information on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  can improve forecast accuracy. The main findings are :\par \par \uc2 The monetary model generally outperforms the na\u239\'00\'EFve model. This negates the findings of the seminal study by Meese and Rogoff (1983) that finds that models which are based on economic fundamentals cannot outperform a naive random walk model.\par \par \uc2 The result that it is possible to beat the na\u239\'00\'EFve model may be due to the fact that the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may help to curb volatility arising due to demand-supply mismatch and stabilise the exchange rate. The exchange rate policy of the Reserve Bank is guided by the need to reduce excess volatility.\par \par \uc2 Forecast accuracy can be improved by extending the monetary model to include forward premium, volatility of capital inflows and order flow.\par \par \uc2 Information on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  helps to improve forecasts at the longer end.\par \par \uc2 Bayesian vector autoregressive models generally outperform their corresponding VAR variants.\par \par \uc2 Thus, the availability of information on certain key variables at regular intervals that affect the exchange rate can lead to a more informed view about the behavior of the future exchange rates which may allow the market participants to plan their \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  exposure better by hedging them appropriately. Such key variables could include past data on exchange rates, forward premia, capital flows, turnover and \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by central banks. As regards availability of data on key variables relating to the Indian \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, most of the data are available in public domain and can easily be accessed by market participants, academicians and professional researchers. Skilful use of these variables will help them gain sound insight into future exchange rate movements.\par \par \uc2 [Note: Development Research Group (DRG) is constituted in the Reserve Bank\u8217\'20\'19s Department of Economic Analysis and Policy for undertaking effective policy-oriented research backed by strong analytical and empirical basis on subjects of current interests. The views expressed in these studies are those of the authors and do not reflect the views of the Reserve Bank.]\par \par \uc2 [Editorial queries for this story should be sent to newswire@enpublishing.co.uk]\par \par \uc2 Dokument ENPNEW0020100226e62q000bi\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
