\par\par \b \uc2 FUND VIEW- India, Korea attractive ,Indonesia a crowded trade\b0\par\par\uc2 510 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Februar 2010\par 09:34\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Rupee, won good \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  picks on fundamentals - Aberdeen\par \par \uc2 * Buying Indonesian bonds is a crowded trade - Aberdeen\par \par \uc2 By Saikat Chatterjee\par \par \uc2 HONG KONG, Feb 11 (Reuters) - Markets may be pricing in too much policy tightening in countries such as South Korea and India, creating opportunities for bond investors, a fund manager at \uc2 Aberdeen Asset Management\uc2  said on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 Central banks will also start withdrawing stimulus by draining excess liquidity, which meant less aggressive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to limit \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  gains, making some of them, including the Indian rupee <INR=>, a good pick, Adam McCabe told Reuters in an interview.\par \par \uc2 "We still think policymakers will go gradually and will focus on domestic demand and will keep in mind their vulnerabilities," said McCabe, who manages $4.4 billion in assets in Asia-focused funds.\par \par \uc2 He added that India and Korea were some of his favourites.\par \par \uc2 The Bank of Korea had once been tipped to be among the first G20 central banks to start tightening policy but the government's opposition to such a move and heightened uncertainty about the global outlook has scaled back those expectations. It left rates unchanged at a review on Thursday. [ID:nTOE61A00S].\par \par \uc2 McCabe said \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  could ill-afford to keep \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  down as it would flood the banking system with rupees, adding fuel to an already high inflation unless it mopped up excess cash, which in turn would add to borrowing costs.\par \par \uc2 Singapore-based McCabe, who moved to Aberdeen from \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2  in 2009, said his fund had a long-duration strategy on Korea and was underweight Thailand, where the market has been underpricing the extent of tightening by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 McCabe was also bullish on Malaysia, expecting it to lag the rest of Asia in raising interest rates and said he favoured the Korean won <KRW=> against the Japanese yen <JPY=> given that South Korean companies were gaining the share of China's market at the expense of their Japanese rivals.\par \par \uc2 Latest data showed South Korea's exports to China in January nearly doubled from a year-ago period. [ID:nSEW002162].\par \par \uc2 CROWDED TRADES\par \par \uc2 But unlike some of his counterparts, McCabe is cautious on Indonesian bonds and has in fact cut positions recently as foreign investors made a beeline for its high-yielding debt.\par \par \uc2 Latest data showed foreigners owned a record 117.5 trillion rupiah worth of federal debt early in February after boosting their holdings by $2.2 billion or nearly a fifth last year.\par \par \uc2 McCabe said that even though the medium term outlook for the economy was positive, a risk of a spike in inflation if the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  kept rates on hold for too long or a lack of progress in reforms could shatter investor confidence.\par \par \uc2 "Foreigners love the place and it worries me when a trade is so crowded as all it needs is a policy mistake for the herd to head to the door," McCabe said. (Editing by Tomasz Janowski)\par \par \uc2 FUND VIEW/ABERDEEN|LANGEN|ABN|M|D|IF|SNI   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020100214e62b00051\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
