\par\par \b \uc2 WRAPUP 1-Asian c.banks scramble to contain  \highlight22\uc2 currency \highlight\uc2  strength\b0\par\par\uc2 594 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Januar 2010\par 11:28\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  seen in S.Korea, Indonesia, \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Singapore\par \par \uc2 * Korean official vows further action on won, if necessary\par \par \uc2 * Concerns that rapid \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  gains may hurt recovery\par \par \uc2 By Kevin Yao\par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE, Jan 11 (Reuters) - Several Asian central banks \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Monday in an effort to temper the rally in their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  as the dollar weakened broadly, highlighting fears that rapid \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rises may hurt economic recovery.\par \par \uc2 Central banks in South Korea, Indonesia, India and Singapore were spotted buying U.S. dollars to contain their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , driven up by the dollar's weakness and expectations that China's strong exports data may prod Beijing to allow the yuan to rise.\par \par \uc2 "I think Asian central banks are watching the pace of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciation," said Thio Chin Loo, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2  in Singapore. "They are cautiously optimistic about the economy and don't want to prick the nascent recovery."\par \par \uc2 In a sign that concerns over strengthening \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were not unique for emerging markets, the head of \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2  said on Monday that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will fight any excessive rise of the Swiss franc against the euro, hinting at a possible \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In Seoul, a senior finance ministry official, speaking after the authorities were seen buying dollars, warned that the authorities would act further to temper the won if necessary.\par \par \uc2 "We are worried that the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market is leaning excessively one way," Kim Ik-joo, head of the ministry's international finance bureau, told Reuters.\par \par \uc2 "We are closely watching \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates and we will take proper measures if necessary," Kim said.\par \par \uc2 Dealers said, the authorities tried to prevent the won from rising past the 1,200-per dollar level, though the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  briefly hit 1,114.9 per dollar, its strongest in more than 15 months, and closed on the stronger side of the 1,200 mark.\par \par \uc2 The won has gained 4 percent against the dollar so far this month, making it the top performer among nine emerging Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  tracked by Reuters.\par \par \uc2 The rising won also intensified purchases of Korean bonds by foreigners, along with the view that rate rises will be slower coming in the year ahead, with the buying, in turn, pushing the won further up.\par \par \uc2 Confronted with a steady stream of investment funds betting that Asia will continue to lead the global economic upturn, the region's authorities have been using both verbal and market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to cool of the pressure on their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Taiwan went a step further last week, unveiling new controls to curb speculative inflows, giving foreign short-term investors betting on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  gains a week to invest their funds in stocks or pull out.\par \par \uc2 In Indonesia, the authorities were also spotted trying to stem the rise of the high-yielding rupiah, which jumped as much as 1 percent on Monday, and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said it would keep on buying dollars to rein in its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In Singapore, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was also seen buying dollars as the Singapore dollar hit a one-month high and in India, state-run banks were spotted buying the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  after the rupee rose 1 percent to its highest in more than 15 months.\par \par \uc2 The rupiah has gained 3 percent against the dollar so far this month, making it the second best performer in Asia. (Additional reporting by Vidya Ranganathan in SINGAPORE, Lee Soo-jung in SEOUL and Swati Bhat in MUMBAI; Editing by Tomasz Janowski)\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-ASIA/\b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2  (WRAPUP 1)|LANGEN|ABX|BNX|FUN   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020100111e61b000ng\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
