\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: India Call Rates Inch Up But Tipped Stable N/T\b0\par\par\uc2 1,945 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 Dezember 2009\par 08:07\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0707 GMT [Dow Jones] India call money market rates have started inching up on rekindled inflation worries, recent hawkish statements from authorities, but excess liquidity in system likely to keep upmove in check near term, says J.Moses Harding, head of global markets group at \f28 \uc2 \f2 IndusInd Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2 ; says, higher demand for funds on shift into new reporting fortnight (Dec.5-Dec.18) would add to upward pressure. "While the outflows from week-on-week auction may not be a concern, advance tax outflows in the third week of December should push call rate higher into 3.40-3.50%," Harding says; still, RBI would not hike rates in hurry as widening interest rate differential will accelerate flow of off-shore funds, which will add more liquidity; tips overnight call rate steady around 3.30% (now at 3.10%) this session. (HAR)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0700 GMT [Dow Jones] KLCI sustains losses, down 0.1% at 1270.75 led by declines in construction, consumer related, banking stocks but dealers say selling pressure muted. "Profit-taking into strength is keeping the market in negative territory but volume traded is very thin. The composition of market players is biased towards retail investors and day traders looking for trading opportunities. The Index may continue to drift within a tight range of 1265-1275 for the rest of the day," dealer at bank-backed brokerage says. Handful of government-linked companies, index heavyweights buck trend on mild support from local funds though momentum remains weak. Among decliners, CIMB (1023.KU) down 0.6% at MYR12.90, 3A-Resources (0012.KU) down 2.8% at MYR1.41, WCT (9679.KU) down 2.7% at MYR2.56. On upside, \uc2 LCL Corp\uc2  (7177.KU) +12.9% at 35 sen, Ranhill (5030.KU) +8.0% at 87.5 sen and Genting (3182.KU) +1.8% at MYR7.36. (VGB)\par \par \uc2 0653 GMT [Dow Jones] China shares turn higher with banks leading gains after China banking regulator says China has asked big banks to raise minimum capital adequacy ratio to 11%, lower than 13% market consensus. Shanghai Composite Index last +1.3% at 3308.14 vs intraday low of 3214.84, likely to drift around 3300, analysts say; "11% is better than previously-talked 13%, which to some extent eases banks capital pressure," says \uc2 Everbright Securities\uc2  analyst Shen Wei; banks also boosted after \uc2 Bank of China Ltd\uc2 . (3988.HK) \uc2 Vice President Zhang Yanling\uc2  says Central Huijin Investment may consider ways to boost the lender's capital base. Bank of China (601988.SH) +2.9% at CNY4.28, \uc2 China Construction Bank\uc2  (601939.SH) +3.5% at CNY6.18. Shenzhen Index down 1.7% at 1213.10.(JIN)\par \par \uc2 0644 GMT [Dow Jones] HSI narrows losses, now down 0.5% at 22,4438.37 vs 22,240.86 midday, after Shanghai Composite turns higher, now +1.5%. While Dubai World's debt problems turned out to be pretty much confined, Daiwa notes Western European markets captured US$2.3 billion, or 71%, of total global funds inflow for week ending Dec. 2, while inflows into global emerging markets dropped further to US$449 million, from US$1.9 billion a week earlier, suggesting "investors' risk appetite is now much lower after the Dubai World debacle." \uc2 Li & Fung\uc2  (0494.HK) worst-performing blue chip, down 3.8% at HK$33.25 after U.S. retailers reported tough November. Mainland developers continue outperformance, with China Overseas Land (0688.HK) +1.4% at HK$18.00, Citic Pacific (0267.HK), with exposure to mainland property market, +1.9% at HK$21.80, as sector plays rise. Market volume solid at HK$44.45 billion.(RLI)\par \par \uc2 0631 GMT [Dow Jones] ASIAN SUMMARY: Stock markets mostly lower as demand depressed by weak Wall Street lead, some caution ahead of U.S. payrolls data due later in global day; Nikkei holds own, +0.4% as worries about strong JPY continues to ease, while \uc2 Japan Airlines\uc2  surges on corporate action as \uc2 AMR Corp\uc2 .'s \uc2 American Airlines\uc2  offers to invest $1.1 billion in ailing JAL along with private-equity firm TPG, trumping previous offer from DeltaAir; Kospi +0.5%, while Shanghai Composite +0.4%; elsewhere S&P/ASX 200 off 1.5%, HSI down 1.4%, STI down 0.5%, PSEI off 0.9%, KLCI flat, Taiex off 0.4%, Thai shares off 1.0%, NZX-50 off 0.2%. \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets in tight ranges as investors await cues from U.S. jobs data; weaker equities slightly depress risk-sensitive \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  with EUR/USD last 1.5050 vs 1.5075 in late NY trade, EUR/JPY 132.76 vs 132.96, USD/JPY 88.19 vs 88.22; Japan Deputy Prime Minister Kan's jawboning of JPY lower had little impact, though comments reinforce policymakers' concerns about JPY's potential rebound; Kan tells news conference he wants JPY to weaken "a bit more." On data front, final reading of Korea's 3Q GDP shows growth at 3.2% vs 2.9% initial estimate, which spurs talk BOK might raise rates earlier than expected; Philippines November CPI +2.8% on-year vs +1.6% in October, fastest pace in 6 months though not expected to prod \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  into tightening rates near term. Spot gold eases back a little to $1,202/oz, down $5.50 from NY. Nymex January crude oil at $75.95/bbl, down 51 cents. (SHN)\par \par \uc2 0628 GMT [Dow Jones] HK short-selling +15.1% at HK$1.682 billion at interval, 4.1% of market total when excluding impact from new listing Futong (0465.HK), unchanged vs Thursday half-day, suggests further falls in HSI (1.4% decline after 4-session 6.7% rise) likely limited. A50 (2823.HK) leads with HK$177.45 million, ICBC (1398.HK) with HK$146.17 million, Ping An (2318.HK) with HK$114.79 million, China Life (2628.HK) with HK$98.65 million and \uc2 BYD Co\uc2 . (1211.HK) with HK$82.31 million amid stock's outperformance (+1.7% midday) after media reports say BYD has received CNY15 billion in credit from Bank of China (3988.HK). (SUT)\par \par \uc2 0618 GMT [Dow Jones] With all the "will they, won't they" talk of late about whether Japanese authorities will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to sell yen, it's worth considering if any market action would have long-lasting impact. We are some way from any action to sell yen, and it has anyway fallen back in recent days, but there's no doubt authorities are slowly and carefully calibrating their rhetoric, and there is political pressure to limit Japan's deflationary doldrums. So what would happen if Tokyo were indeed to act? It would probably have to go into market on its own; the Europeans are unlikely to stump up for USD buying and U.S. has no stomach for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Unilateral \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would require Tokyo to go into market hard. There's no doubt markets would get a fright if that happened, in part given it's been so long between \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  for Tokyo; you'd expect at least 24 hours of fairly large-scale volatility, depending on how many times Tokyo went in, at what times and in what size. But after that it would not be surprising to see the impact fade, and for markets to start testing their luck in pushing yen higher. Tokyo theoretically could dump an infinite amount of yen on market; that however could get costly (it would have to pay interest on bills it'd likely sell to mop up the extra yen in money market). And while Japan has the capacity to act, the market is still much bigger than it is. See =MONEY TALKS: What If Japan Were To \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  On The Yen? (RXM)\par \par \uc2 0614 GMT [Dow Jones] \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  6.90% 2019 bond yield at auction cutoff price likely 7.40% vs 7.37% now, according to median estimate in Dow Jones poll of five market participants; 7.02% 2016 bond's cutoff yield likely 7.31%, 8.28% 2032 likely 8.315%, poll shows. RBI to auction INR30 billion of 2016 note, INR40 billion of 2019 note, INR30 billion of 2032 bond later in session. "Considering the selloff this week, the cutoffs should be bullish as most traders have positioned themselves lightly to buy," says state-run bank's dealer; but adds, market may not run too much despite likely good auction, as uncertainty lingers over monetary moves, many will eye industrial output, inflation data--both due next week. (HAR)\par \par \uc2 0611 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan government bonds end marginally higher as foreign banks buy on anticipation auction of NT$40 billion 10-year bonds to be well-received Thursday, says local-bank trader. Notes volume, thinnest for key 10-year bond since when-issued trade began late November, suggests caution among local investors prior to auction, therefore more willing to put money in shorter-dated five-year issue, of which volume more than doubles. Expects yields trapped in tight range next week as investors await clearer direction from auction results. Reopened 10-year yield finishes at 1.4475% vs 1.4522% last close, tipped in 1.4400%-1.4460% band Monday; 5-year yield ends at 0.8800% vs 0.8930% last close, tipped in 0.8700%-0.8900% band.(CYH)\par \par \uc2 0602 GMT [Dow Jones] Nikkei closes up 0.5% at 10,022.59, its intraday high, and 1st close above 10,000 level since Oct. 30; "Given yesterday's big gains, the Nikkei could have fallen on profit-taking, but foreigners seem to be buying blue chips and supporting the index," says Mizuho Securities market analyst Yukio Takahashi. Says sentiment improving thanks to Bank of Japan's monetary easing measures, but for Nikkei to test higher ground, market would want to see more positive economic indicators and possibly further government measures against deflation and yen strength. Says expects Nikkei to move in 9700-10,200 range next week. (JUO)\par \par \uc2 0555 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/JPY may target 100 next year vs last 88.15, says \uc2 Morgan Stanley\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  research team, as "we see fewer reasons to sell the dollar/yen as current levels favor buying the pair." Notes also, risks of Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  now higher, as "we think that Japanese authorities will want to resist yen strength around the Y85 area, given that a break of this level sets up a test of all-time lows in the dollar/yen around Y80." But adds, Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  won't work much until more of world's central banks begin tightening. MS expects central banks to start hiking rates from 2Q next year. (TMO) [ 12-04-09 0207ET ]\par \par \uc2 0548 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan shares end down 0.4% at 7,650.91; policy-sensitive stocks lower, with financials down 1.1%, builders down 0.7% on profit-taking ahead of tomorrow's elections of local city and county heads, says Capital Securities assistant VP Diana Wu. Tips Taiex in 7,550-7,750 band Monday; suggests investors to take cues from November results announcements by tech names next week. Still, notes upside of Taiex limited next week as Tingyi's (0322.HK) NT$17.1 billion TDRs may soak up some liquidity. Chinatrust Financial (2891.TW) down 2.0% at NT$19.45, Shining Building (5531.TW) down 2.2% at NT$36.10. Taiex ends +2.1% for the week. (WUM) [ 12-04-09 0207ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020091204e5c40004h\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
