\par\par \b \uc2 Reuters Summit-UPDATE 1-BofA-Merrill fears India reform delays\b0\par\par\uc2 827 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 November 2009\par 10:04\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Reuters Limited    \par \par \uc2 (For other news from the Reuters India Investment Summit, click on {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.reuters.com/summit/IndiaInvestment09?pid=500"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.reuters.com/summit/IndiaInvestment09?pid=500}}}\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 * Conservative regulations may delay financial reforms\par \par \uc2 * Volatile fund flows worrying but taxing them not a solution\par \par \uc2 * India should step up domestic participation in stk mkt (Adds details, more quotes)\par \par \uc2 By Nishant Kumar and Pratish Narayanan\par \par \uc2 MUMBAI, Nov 23 (Reuters) - India, whose protected financial sector helped insulate it from the worst of the global economic meltdown, should not be complacent in its push for financial sector reforms, a top Bank of America-Merrill Lynch executive said on Monday.\par \par \uc2 "One thing that does worry me is that the conservative nature of Indian regulation. That may persist longer than it would otherwise have done because of the financial crisis," said \uc2 Kevan Watts\uc2 , who heads the combined Indian \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \uc2 Bank of America\uc2  and \uc2 Merrill Lynch\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The rousing re-election of the Congress party-led government in May, which freed it from dependence on communist allies, lifted hopes of an acceleration in long-delayed financial reforms.\par \par \uc2 But the global financial meltdown has dampened Indian appetite for financial liberalisation, especially as limits on foreign banks and active regulation of the financial sector were seen as sheltering it from the worst of the downturn.\par \par \uc2 "The danger is, that if you like, India will rest on its laurels of having circumvented the crisis well," Watts told the Reuters India Investment Summit in Mumbai.\par \par \uc2 India has for years talked about allowing a greater role for foreign banks and giving equal voting rights to foreigners in private-sector banks, currently limited to 10 percent irrespective of their actual holding.\par \par \uc2 No foreign firm is allowed to operate in the pension sector and private Indian players have only a limited presence.\par \par \uc2 The life insurance market was opened nine years ago, but companies are not allowed to raise debt or go public in their first decade of \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2 , putting the onus on shareholders to fund costlier growth.\par \par \uc2 Foreign ownership in the life insurance sector is capped at 26 percent.\par \par \uc2 Watts said financial reforms could help India's large savings to make its way into productive investments.\par \par \uc2 India has about $400 billion in domestic savings, but little is funnelled through the banking channel currently to fund, for example, the country's huge requirements to build infrastructure.\par \par \uc2 CAPITAL FLOWS\par \par \uc2 Watts, who has spent nearly three decades with \uc2 Merrill Lynch\uc2  and has been based in India for about 18 months, said swings in foreign capital flows were a concern, but taxing them was not a solution.\par \par \uc2 "I think volatility of fund flows is certainly something I would be worrying about if I was advising the government," said Watts, who early in his career worked for the British treasury.\par \par \uc2 "When you start taxing things, there are a lot of unintended consequences," the Oxford alumnus said, adding, "Critically for India, any unilateral move of that kind would really damage its reputation globally."\par \par \uc2 He said India should instead use existing mechanisms, such as sterilising inflows by issuing special bonds. \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has in the past used market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  bonds to absorb excess liquidity generated by large foreign capital inflows.\par \par \uc2 Foreign investors have so far bought more than $15 billion of local equities in 2009, after selling $13 billion in 2008, helping send Indian stocks up about 75 percent and lifting the rupee to its highest in more than a year.\par \par \uc2 The capital inflows are part of a trend globally that is seeing investors chasing faster growing emerging markets, given sluggish recovery and low interest rates in developed economies.\par \par \uc2 This has raised concerns inflows may create asset price bubbles and drive \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to uncompetitive levels. That has prompted some economies, including Brazil and Taiwan, to impose controls and others, such as Russia, to say they were considering such steps.\par \par \uc2 Watts said India should also encourage more domestic participation in its equity markets to counter the impact of foreign investment flows.\par \par \uc2 Indian institutional investors lack muscle to counter rapid fund flows. For instance, government pension funds are not allowed to invest in equities, while investment by private pension funds is limited to just 5 percent of their assets.\par \par \uc2 Financial reform is "not about whether foreigners can own 49 percent of life insurance joint ventures or 26 percent," he said. "Perhaps the most important thing that the Indian government can do is to encourage the further development of domestic Indian institutional investors," he said. (For summit blog: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://blogs.reuters.com/summits/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://blogs.reuters.com/summits/}}}\uc2 ) (For all summit stories: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://in.reuters.com/news/globalcoverage/summit"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://in.reuters.com/news/globalcoverage/summit}}}\uc2 ) . (Editing by Jean Yoon) ((nishant.kumar@thomsonreuters.com; +91-22-6636 9247; Reuters Messaging: nishant.kumar.reuters.com@reuters.net)) ((If you have a query or comment on this story, send an email to news.feedback.asia@thomsonreuters.com))\par \par \uc2 INDIA-SUMMIT/MERRILL (UPDATE 1)|LANGEN|C|D|E|M|O|U|MTL|GRO|SOF|OIL|SNS|SNI    \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020091123e5bn0011q\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
