\par\par \b \uc2 MONEY TALKS: Why The RBI May Let The Rupee Rise\b0\par\par\uc2 By Subhadip Sircar \par \uc2 A Dow Jones Newswires Column \par \uc2 638 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 Oktober 2009\par 10:56\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 MUMBAI (Dow Jones)--"The day before yesterday's volatility is today's flexibility." So quipped then-\uc2 Reserve Bank of India\uc2  chief Yaga Venugopal Reddy during the rupee's sharp rise of 2007.\par \par \uc2 Once again, the rupee is on a roll, hitting over one year-highs against the U.S. dollar and breaching key technical levels. It has finally broken free after trailing its Asian peers and has gained nearly 4% so far this month, while the Philippine peso has risen 2.2%, the Korean won 1.2% and Indonesian rupiah 2.7%. Yet the \uc2 Reserve Bank of India\uc2  has looked away.\par \par \uc2 This is at variance with their counterparts in places like South Korea, Taiwan and Thailand who have been active in their market, buying dollars to protect their exports.\par \par \uc2 Going by analysts forecasts, the rupee is set to rise further from here. Standard Chartered Bank has a dollar/rupee forecast of INR45 by end-March and INR42 by the end of the fiscal 2010-2011 year, while \uc2 ING Vysya Bank\uc2  has a one-year forecast of INR43. Currently it is trading at INR46.24.\par \par \uc2 There are seven reasons the RBI may let the rupee gain, only stepping in to iron out the moves if they become too sharp.\par \par \uc2 For one thing, the imperative to protect exports is less for India, which is largely a domestic consumption-driven economy.\par \par \uc2 Secondly, the rupee is to a large extent playing catch up to other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ; the RBI has never indicated it likes an undervalued \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and may not hold the rupee back if the U.S. dollar collapses against its key trading partner \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Thirdly, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has traditionally shied away from \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  as long as the dollar's decline has been broad based, which is the case now.\par \par \uc2 Fourthly, top government policy makers are increasingly getting vocal about the need to continue stimulus until growth takes hold.\par \par \uc2 The RBI is expected to be one of the first major Asian central banks to raise interest rates as it battles inflation pressures, but allowing the rupee to rise would temper imported inflation and could allow the RBI to push back the hikes a bit. Global crude oil prices have climbed to one-year highs and comprise two-third of India's import costs.\par \par \uc2 Fifthly, what worries the RBI is the quality of capital inflows, less so the quantity.\par \par \uc2 While portfolio inflows have may a comeback on the back of a global risk appetite revival, the current capital inflows also include a healthy dose of foreign direct investment, overseas borrowing and remittances from abroad. The RBI can live with that.\par \par \uc2 Sixthly, \uc2 Westpac\uc2  says that the cost of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  is among the highest in Asia. The government would have to bear an interest cost for the special bonds the RBI would have to issue to soak up liquidity. Given the government's stretched finances, this would be an added burden.\par \par \uc2 Seventhly, top policy makers seem increasingly comfortable with a rising rupee as long as volatility is kept in check, going by their recent comments at least.\par \par \uc2 (Subhadip Sircar is Senior Correspondent for Dow Jones Newswires covering central banking, fixed income and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets in India. He can be contacted on 91-022-61456113 or by email: subhadip.sircar@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 TALK BACK: We invite readers to send us comments on this or other financial news topics. Please email us at TalkbackAsia@dowjones.com. Readers should include their full names, work or home addresses and telephone numbers for verification purposes. We reserve the right to edit and publish your comments along with your name; we reserve the right not to publish reader comments. [ 16-10-09 0856GMT ]\par \par \uc2 1129 1171 5008 70779 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020091016e5ag000rs\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
