\par\par \b \uc2 Telkom, miners lift S.African bourse; rand gains\b0\par\par\uc2 721 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Oktober 2009\par 18:00\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Stocks up, led by telecoms group Telkom\par \par \uc2 * Rand gains, flirts with 14-month high vs dollar\par \par \uc2 JOHANNESBURG, Oct 12 (Reuters) - South African stocks rose on Monday, boosted by higher global markets and with telecoms group Telkom jumping on reports the government plans to force down the interconnect costs charged by cell phone operators.\par \par \uc2 The rand rallied, flirting with a 14-month high against the dollar, as the greenback slipped and metal prices gained.\par \par \uc2 The JSE Top-40 index of blue chips <.JTOPI> added 0.92 percent to 22,936.15 points, also buoyed by above-forecast corporate earnings that lifted investor sentiment globally and the strong metal prices that bolstered miners.\par \par \uc2 The broader All-share index <.JALSH> added 0.99 percent to 25,630.16 points.\par \par \uc2 "Everyone is looking at the earnings season in America and Europe for signs of a sustained recovery in the global economy," said Michael Carlsson, a trader at Consilium Securities.\par \par \uc2 "So far so good. But the big heavyweights are coming out this week and we'll see how it goes."\par \par \uc2 On the bourse, Telkom was among the winners on news that the government may force cell phone companies to cut interconnect fees. [ID:nLF553811]\par \par \uc2 Shares in Africa's biggest fixed phone line operator rose 3.15 percent to 42.86 rand.\par \par \uc2 "Telkom stands to gain from lower interconnect fees and the mobile operators will battle. I think we are seeing that sort of being reflected in the shares," said Jan Meintjes, an analyst at Gryphon Asset Management.\par \par \uc2 The rand <ZAR=D3> was trading at 7.3410 to the dollar at 1550 GMT, 0.9 percent stronger than its previous close in New York after touching 7.30 earlier in the session.\par \par \uc2 Traders said a weak dollar helped propel metals, sending the price of gold -- a key South African export that is often seen as an alternative to holding the greenback -- above $1,050 an ounce and towards Friday's record high.\par \par \uc2 The dollar fell as investors positioned ahead of U.S. corporate earnings his week on expectations that better-than-forecast results will drive up risk appetite.\par \par \uc2 "Gold is flying higher, oil is up, those things will drive commodity \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  like the rand stronger," Bidvest Bank chief trader Ion de Vleeschauwer said.\par \par \uc2 "The dollar is very weak, so again its the normal factors influencing the rand that is in play."\par \par \uc2 The rand has surged almost 30 percent against the greenback this year, but moves have lost momentum around 7.30 -- it hit a 13-month high of 7.2925 last month.\par \par \uc2 It has recovered all the losses sparked by the collapse of merger talks between mobile phone group MTN and \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \uc2 Bharti Airtel\uc2  <BRTI>BO> and despite repeated verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , warning it is too strong.\par \par \uc2 Analysts say dollar and commodity price moves continue to drive the market, although some traders suggest the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has upped buying dollars and that importers tend to come in around 7.30 to the dollar, capping gains.\par \par \uc2 "The flavour of the week, of the month, or the year really, is the weak dollar and it looks to be continuing," de Vleeschauwer said. Government bonds were relatively steady, with investors nervous about a big increase in government and utility supply, as well fear expectations electricity prices will rise sharply for a number of years, spurring inflation.\par \par \uc2 The yield, which moves inversely to the price, on the 2015 issue <ZAR157=> up 1 basis point at 8.43 percent while the 2036 <ZAR209=> yield rose 3 basis points to 8.62 percent.\par \par \uc2 On the bourse, \uc2 Old Mutual\uc2  jumped 3.92 percent to 12.73 rand, making it the top gainer among blue chips, after it said it planned to issue a seven-year sterling benchmark bond.\par \par \uc2 Miners also gained as commodity prices cruised higher with Anglo Platinum up 3.05 percent to 710 rand and rival \uc2 Impala Platinum\uc2  tacked on 1.75 percent to 174.50 rand.\par \par \uc2 But Sasol shed 0.28 percent to 288 rand after broker \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  cut the petrochemical group's profit forecast and its 12-month price target. (Reporting by Gordon Bell, Vuyani Ndaba and Tiisetso Motsoeneng)\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-SAFRICA/CLOSE|LANGEN|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|AFN|SF|RNP|DNP|PTD|PMF   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020091012e5ac0018c\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
