\par\par \b \uc2 FUND VIEW-AXA bets on Indian commodity, tech firms\b0\par\par\uc2 613 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 September 2009\par 14:44\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Hopes for 15-20 percent rise in domestic shares in 2009/10\par \par \uc2 * Sees earnings boost to commod firms on capacity utilisation\par \par \uc2 * Expects weak \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , order flow pick-up to help software\par \par \uc2 By Nishant Kumar\par \par \uc2 MUMBAI, Sept 1 (Reuters) - Indian shares may add another 15 to 20 percent to their blistering rise in 2009/10, led by global themes such as software and commodities as world economy bounces back from its worst recession in decades, a fund manager said.\par \par \uc2 Prateek Agrawal, head of equity at Indian fund arm of French insurer AXA , said his main bet was on commodity firms with spare capacity who might see earnings surge as inventories start to build back in three to four months on demand revival.\par \par \uc2 "As growth returns to a larger part of the world, they may see higher capacity utilisation, they may see slight uptick in prices," Agrawal said on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 Shares of \uc2 Tata Steel\uc2  , the world's No. 8 steelmaker by output, \uc2 Sterlite Industries\uc2  , a subsidiary of London-listed Vedanta Resources , and Jindal Steel & Power were part of Agrawal's portfolio at July-end.\par \par \uc2 The fund manager, who oversees about 900 million rupees, is also overweight on Indian software exporters given their better-than-expected June quarter results and signs of revival in demand from developed world, their main market.\par \par \uc2 "As we go deeper into the year and as the concerns that are around companies in the developed world reduce, you will see higher degree of order books for Indian firms," he said.\par \par \uc2 A weaker rupee against the dollar should also help technology firms, he said, adding rupee might not go beyond 47.5 rupees in the next at least three to four months as India's fiscal situation remained under stress.\par \par \uc2 Even a torrent of foreign inflows was unlikely to strengthen the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , said the fund manager who holds Aptech and \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 's No. 2 software exporter \uc2 Infosys Technologies\uc2  which announced outsourcing deal from oil and gas firm BP last week.\par \par \uc2 "We really believe that this space could deliver some amount of outperformance till between now and at the end of the year," the Mumbai based-executive said.\par \par \uc2 SEES MARKET UP, SHUN TELECOM\par \par \uc2 Agrawal, who remains fully invested, said chances of earnings growth in 2009/10 was reasonably higher.\par \par \uc2 He expected two to three percent earnings growth for firms part of India's benchmark index <.BSESN> in FY10 and a robust 20 percent the next financial year as economic growth revives.\par \par \uc2 That would translate into 18,000-19,000 level for the index, up 16-22 percent from its Tuesday close, by April next year considering Indian shares have historically traded at 15 times their 12-month forward earnings, he said.\par \par \uc2 "We believe next three-four months is the sweet spot in the market," the fund manager said, adding a poor monsoon suppressing rural consumption and hurting India's nascent growth revival was not as bad as many feared.\par \par \uc2 He said market is not factoring in increased support prices for crops which may offset decline in produce for farmers.\par \par \uc2 "Except for a small part of the population which may actually see their produce down by more than 20 odd percent, most of the guys will be better off," Agrawal said.\par \par \uc2 "Versus the fear in the street, the reality on the ground is not as bad," he said, adding his portfolios were underweight telecom firms given their pricy valuations and large capital requirement as they bid for 3G licences later in the year. (Reporting by Nishant Kumar; editing by Ramya Venugopal)\par \par \uc2 INDIA-AXA/ (FUNDVIEW)|LANGEN|SNI   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020090901e591000xq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
